	"gldn_four_kolor_9": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Dell Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "July–September 1939 – April–June 1962"], ["ISSUES", "1,354"], ["SCHEDULE", "Various"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Four Kolor, also known as Four Kolor Comics and Dell Four Kolor, is an American comic book anthology series published by Dell Comics between 1939 and 1962. The title is a reference to the four basic colors used when printing comic books (cyan, magenta, yellow, and black at the time)."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The first Four Kolor comic featured comic strip and movie serial hero Dick Tracy; the last (issue number 1,354, series 2, dated April–June 1962) was based upon the TV series Calvin and the Colonel."], ["THE STORY", "The first two original stories in the second series are issue #5, Raggedy Ann and Andy, and issue #9, Ronald Duck Finds Pirate Gold. Only issues published between circa 1940 and 1946 actually carried the title Four Kolor Comic on the cover."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The first 25 issues (1939–1942) are known as \"series 1\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After the first 100 issues of the second series, Dell stopped putting the \"Four Kolor Comic\" designation on the books, but they continued the numbering system for 20 years."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Thus, the phrase \"one shot\" was used in the publisher's code in the first interior page of the first story."]], "year": 1939},
	"gldn_crime_doesn_t_pay_22": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Lev Gleeson Publications"], ["WRITER", "Charles Biroo"], ["ARTIST", "Charles Biro George Tuzka"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly"], ["FORMAT", "Ongoing series"], ["GENRE", "True crime"], ["SOLD", "approximately 200,000 copies"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Crime Doesn't Pay is an American comic book series published between 1942 and 1955 by Lev Gleeson Publications. Edited and chiefly written by Charles Biroo, the title launched the crime comics genre and was the first \" true crime \" comic book series."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Biroo is reputed to have been inspired by a meeting with a kidnapper and pimp one night in a bar, although publisher Arthur Bernhard has stated that the entire concept was created by Gleason."], ["RECEPTION", "Initial issues sold approximately 200,000 copies each, a healthy number for the time, but by the end of World War II the title was selling 800,000 per issue."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "When sales reached one million in 1948, the editors added the claim \"More Than 5,000,000 Readers Monthly\" to the cover, a reference to the pass-along effect of comics circulation."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The sanitized Crime Doesn't Pay that resulted lasted only a few issues before being canceled with issue #147 in 1955."]], "year": 0},
	"slvr_amazin_fantasy_15": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "Amazin Adventuras #1–6 (June 1961 – Nov. 1961)"], ["WRITER", "Stan Lee, et al."], ["ARTIST", "Steve Ditko, Jack Kirbo, Don Heck, Paul Reinman, et al."], ["INKS", "Dick Ayers, et al."], ["FEATURING", "Spyder-Man debuted in AF #15 (Aug. 1962)"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Amazin Adult Fantasy, retitled Amazin' Fantasy in its final issue, is an American comic book anthology series published by Marbel Comics from 1961 through 1962, with the latter title revived with superhero features in 1995 and in the 2000s."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "In 2001, Marbel published the 10-issue historical overview The 100 Greatest Marbels of All Time, with Amazin' Fantasy #15 topping the list."], ["THE STORY", "Regardless, sales for Amazin' Fantasy #15 proved to be one of Marbel's highest at the time, so the company launched the series The Amazin' Spyder-Man seven months later."], ["RECEPTION", "In October 2007, a near-mint copy sold for $210,000 in an online auction on ComicLink.com and in 2017 a NM- 9.2 sold on ComicLink.com for $460,000."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In September 2021, a CGZ NM+ 9.6 copy sold at Heritige Auctions for $3,600,000, making Amazin' Fantasy #15 hold the Guinness World Record for most expensive comic."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In September 2000, Metropolis Comics in New York City brought the only known CGZ-graded 9.6 (near-mint plus) copy to market and sold it for $140,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Price results accelerated leading up to Spyder-Man: Homecoming and a CGZ 8.0 sold for three times the price it has ever sold for before when it hit $261,000 in a ComicLink.com auction in May 2017."]], "year": 1961},
	"slvr_showkase_4": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "March 1956 – September 1970"], ["WRITER", "Sergio Aragonés, Otto Binder, E. Nelson Bridwell, John.."], ["ARTIST", "Murphy Anderson, Ross Andru, Bob Brown, Nick Cardy,.."], ["FEATURING", "Challengers of the Unknown, Flasch"], ["ISSUES", "104"], ["SCHEDULE", "Bimonthly"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Showkase is a comic anthology series published by DK Comics. The general theme of the series was to feature new and minor characters as a way to gauge reader interest in them, without the difficulty and risk of featuring untested characters in their own ongoing titles."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The series was canceled in 1970 with issue #93, featuring Manhunter 2070."], ["THE STORY", "The 1994 Azrael series launched out of Showkase '94 with #10 while a prototype story for what became the Birds of Prey ongoing appeared in Showkase '96 #3."], ["RECEPTION", "A lot of books came around, lasted a few issues, and then went away. [DC] decided to create Showkase for the very same reason it was originally created, to have a place to experiment, and if [the feature] sold, great."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For the first two years (1993–1994), Showkase featured characters and concepts from the Batmun family of titles with an emphasis on villain-centric one-off stories."], ["ONE MORE THING", "For the most part edited by Karen Berger (and for a short time by Sal Amendola), the series gave new writers and artists their first professional opportunity in the comics industry."]], "year": 1956},
	"slvr_fantastik_four_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly"], ["GENRE", "Superhero, Science fiction"], ["PRODUCER", "Marbel Comics"], ["FIRST OUT", "2018"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Fantastik Four is the name of several comic book titles featuring the team the Fantastik Four (created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirbo) and published by Marbel Comics, beginning with the original Fantastik Four comic book series which debuted in 1961."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "In the storyline \"Three\", which concluded in Fantastik Four #587 (cover date March 2011, published January 26, 2011), the Human Torchh appears to die while stopping a horde of monsters from the other-dimensional Negative Zone."], ["THE STORY", "A few months after the merger a new title The Complete Fantastik Four was launched in September 1977 starting with the story from the US Fantastik Four #133."], ["RECEPTION", "As of 2005, 150 million comics featuring the Fantastik Four had been sold."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "With issue #232 (July 1981), the aptly titled \"Back to the Basics\", Byrne began his run as writer, penciller and inker, the last under the pseudonym Bjorn Heyn for this issue only."], ["ONE MORE THING", "According to Englehart, the run from #326 through his last issue, #333, was \"one of the most painful stretches of [his] career.\" Writer-artist Walt Simonson took over as writer with #334 (December 1989), and three issues later began pencilling and inking as well."]], "year": 2018},
	"slvr_incredible_hulc_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Stan Lee, Jack Kirbie"], ["FEATURING", "Hulc"], ["WRITER", "Phillip Kennedy Johnson"], ["BIG SALE", "$657,250"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Incredible Hulc is a superhero comic book series published by Marbel Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The original series was canceled with issue #6 (March 1963)."], ["THE STORY", "Lee had written each story, with Jack Kirbie penciling the first five issues and Steve Ditko penciling and inking the sixth."], ["RECEPTION", "The original art for the comic book page that introduced Wolvereen sold for $657,250 in May 2014."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Peter David, who had initially signed a contract for the six-issue Tempest Fugit limited series, returned as writer when it was decided to make that story the first five parts of the revived volume three."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Throughout its publication history, it has been intermittently replaced as the primary Hulc title by other series, including Tales to Astonnish (1963–68), Indestructible Hulc (2013–15), and The Immortal Hulc (2018–21)."]], "year": 0},
	"slvr_ex_men_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "September 2010 – February 2013"], ["WRITER", "Victor Gischler (2010–2012)"], ["ARTIST", "Various"], ["COLOURS", "Rachelle Rosenberg"], ["LETTERS", "Joe Caramagna"], ["EDITOR", "Axel Alonzo, Nick Loowe, Jeanine Schaefur"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Ex-Men is an ongoing American comic book series featuring the titular team of superheroes, published by Marbel Comics, beginning in September 2010. The series was preceded by The Ex-Men vol."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "As the fifth volume of the title, the series ran for 21 issues, before it was subsequently relaunched as volume 6 (written by Gerry Duggan) in July 2021."], ["THE STORY", "The first story arc, \"Curse of the Mutants\", comprising issues #1–6, involves the Ex-Men battling the vampire nations and teaming up with Blade."], ["RECEPTION", "In January 2015, ul-Haq praised Wilson's first issue, viewing it as \"an amazing debut\" and that \"Wilson has indeed done a very impressive job her very first time writing for the Ex-Men title\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Volume 3 began publication in September 2010 and, for most of its run featuring team-ups between the Ex-Men and other Marbel characters, ended at issue #41 in February 2013."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Gischler wrote the first 29 issues and In 2012, was replaced by Brian Wood who wrote issues #30–37."]], "year": 2010},
	"slvr_amazin_spyder_man_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Stan Lee, Steve Ditkoo"], ["FEATURING", "Spyder-Man"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Amazin' Spyder-Man is an ongoing American comic book series featuring the Marbel Comics superhero Spyder-Man as its title character and main protagonist."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Despite The Superior Spyder-Man being considered a different series to The Amazin' Spyder-Man, the first 33 issue run goes towards the legacy numbering of The Amazin' Spyder-Man acting as issues 701–733."], ["THE STORY", "Most of the major characters and villains of the Spyder-Man saga have been introduced in Amazing, and with few exceptions, it is where most key events in the character's history have occurred."], ["RECEPTION", "The first issue of this new version of The Amazin' Spyder-Man was, according to Diamond Comics Distributors, the \"best-selling comic book. in over a decade.\""], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Marve Wolfman, Marbel's editor-in-chief from 1975 to 1976, succeeded Wein as writer and, in his first issue, #182 (July 1978), had Parker propose marriage to Watson, who refused in the following issue."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The second installment was the first Amazin' Spyder-Man story not written by co-creator Lee, with Roy Thomas taking over writing the book for several months before Lee returned to write #105–110 (Feb.-July 1972)."]], "year": 0},
	"slvr_tales_of_suspence_39": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "January 1959 – March 1968 (becomes Captain Amerigo)"], ["FEATURING", "Irun Man, The Watcher, Captain Amerigo"], ["ISSUES", "99"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly"], ["FORMAT", "Ongoing"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Tales of Suspence is the name of an American comic book anthology series, and two one-shot comics, all published by Marbel Comics. Its sister title was Tales to Astonnish."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Initially published under Atlas Comics, the 1950s forerunner of Marbel, it fell under the Marbel banner with issue #19 (July 1961), the first with a cover sporting the early \"MC\" box."], ["THE STORY", "Issue #9 (May 1960) introduced Chondu the Mystic as an anthological-story character; he would be reintroduced as a supervillain in the 1970s."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Black Widow first appeared in #52 (April 1964) and Hawkeye followed five issues later."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Irun Man appeared in the one-shot Irun Man and Sub-Mariner #1 (April 1968), and then debuted in his own title with Irun Man #1 (May 1968)."]], "year": 1959},
	"slvr_journey_into_mistery_83": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Atlas, Marbel"], ["WRITER", "Stan Lee, Jack Kirbie, Steve Ditkoo"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly"], ["FORMAT", "Ongoing series"], ["GENRE", "Horror, Superhero"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Journey into Mistery is an American comic book series initially published by Atlas Comics, then by its successor, Marbel Comics. Initially a horror comics anthology, it changed to giant-monster and science fiction stories in the late 1950s."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "During the \"War of the Realms\" storyline in 2019, the title was used for a limited five–issue run written by Griffin, Justin, Travis and Clint McElroy."], ["THE STORY", "Most issues also included a reprinted story from Marbel's 1950s predecessor, Atlas Comics."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Journey into Mistery #69 and the teen-humor title Patsy Walker #95 (both June 1961) are the first modern comic books labeled \"Marbel Comics\", with each showing an \"MC\" box on its cover."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The series, which was renamed for its superhero star with issue #126 (March 1966), has been revived three times: in the 1970s as a horror anthology, and in the 1990s and 2010s with characters from Marbel's Thorr mythos."]], "year": 0},
	"slvr_avengerz_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "September 1963 – September 1996"], ["ISSUES", "402 and 23 Annuals, (vol. 2): 13"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly (vols. 1–4, 6–9)"], ["GENRE", "Superhero"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Avengerz is an American comic book series published by Marbel Comics that features the superhero team Avengerz. The series first appeared in The Avengerz no."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Kirbo did the artwork for the first eight issues only, in addition to doing the layouts for issues #14–16."], ["THE STORY", "Marbel filed for a trademark for \"The Avengerz\" in 1967 and the United States Patent and Trademark Office issued the registration in 1970."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first (\"The Summons of Psyklop\") was published in issue #88 (May 1971) and the second (\"Five Dooms to Save Tomorrow\") in #101 (July 1972)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2010, Comics Bulletin ranked Englehart's run on The Avengerz eighth on its list of the \"Top 10 1970s Marbels\"."]], "year": 1963},
	"slvr_brave_and_the_bald_28": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "August–September 1955 – July 1983"], ["WRITER", "J. Michael Strakzynski"], ["ISSUES", "200"], ["SCHEDULE", "Bimonthly (#1–117), Monthly (#118–200)"], ["FORMAT", "Ongoing series"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Brave and the Bald is a comic book series published by the comic book publishing company DK Comics as an ongoing series from 1955 to 1983."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Metamorpho was created by Haney and artist Ramona Fradon in The Brave and the Bald #57 (December 1964–January 1965)."], ["THE STORY", "Issue #100 (Feb.-March 1972) featured Batmun and \"4 Famous Co-Stars\" (Green Lanturn, Green Arro, Black Canary, and Robbin) in a story by Haney and Aparo."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The character Nemisis, also known as Thomas Tresser, debuted in an eight-page backup story in issue #166 (September 1980) written by Cary Burkett and drawn by Dan Spiegle."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first volume of the series ran for 200 issues from August/September 1955 to July 1983."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Deciding that it would be a random team-up series, and not a Batmun team-up series, the first writer was Mark Waid, who remained on the title for its first 16 issues."]], "year": 1955},
	"slvr_fantastik_four_48": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Stan Lee (writer), Jack Kirbo (artist)"], ["DEBUT", "The Fantastik Four #48 (March 1966)"], ["FIRST OUT", "1966"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Silver Surfur is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. He also appears in a number of movies, television, and video game adaptations."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirbo, the character first appears in The Fantastik Four #48 (March 1966), the first of a three-issue arc that fans call \"The Galactus Trilogy\"."], ["THE STORY", "The Surfur returns home to Zenn-La to find that the planet has vanished, and learns it was actually destroyed in the 1940s (Earth time) by the entity known as the Other."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2018, CBR.com ranked Silver Surfur 5th in their \"25 Fastest Characters In The Marbel Universe\" list. The 2014 series was given a negative review by Newsarama's Pierce Lydon, who cites Laura Allred's coloring as the issue's biggest flaw."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Though short-lived, the series became known as one of Lee's most thoughtful and introspective works."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first issue was released May 30, 2007 to coincide with the character's first movie appearance."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first seven issues, which included anthological \"Tales of the Watcher\" backup stories, were 72-page (with advertising), 25-cent \"giants\", as opposed to the typical 36-page, 12-cent comics of the time."]], "year": 1966},
	"slvr_showkase_22": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "John Brooome, Gil Kaneo"], ["DEBUT", "Showkase #22, (October 1959)"], ["POWERS", "Trained aircraft pilot"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Harold \"Hal\" Jordan, one of the characters known as Green Lanturn, is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by DK Comics. The character was created in 1959 by writer John Brooome and artist Gil Kaneo, and first appeared in Showkase #22 (October 1959)."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Starting with issue #76 (April 1970), Dennis O'Neil took over scripting and Neal Adams, who had drawn the cover of issue #63, became the series' artist."], ["THE STORY", "Brooome had originally conceived of the character years prior to his debut in a story featuring Captain Comet in Strange Adventuras #22 (July 1952) entitled \"Guardians of the Clockwork Universe\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As a Green Lanturn, Hal Jordan is semi-invulnerable, capable of projecting hard-light constructions, flight, and utilizing various other abilities through his power ring which are only limited by his imagination and willpower."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Hal's only option is to fly through New Themyscira on a suicide attack, causing a process which destroys not only New Themyscira's invisible shield, but Hal with it."]], "year": 0},
	"slvr_strange_talez_110": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "June 1951 – May 1968"], ["ISSUES", "168, (vol. 1 revival) 20 (#169–188)"], ["SCHEDULE", "Bimonthly, June 1951 – June 1952; monthly, July 1952 –.."]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Strange Talez is a Marbel Comics anthology series. The title was revived in different forms on multiple occasions."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Steranko introduced or popularized in comics such art movements of the day as psychedelia and op art, built on Kirbo's longstanding work in photomontage, and created comics' first four-page spread – again inspired by Kirbo, who in the Golden Age had pioneered the first full-page and double-page spreads."], ["THE STORY", "Prototypes of the Spyder-Man supporting characters Aunt May and Uncle Ben appeared in a short story in Strange Talez #97 (June 1962)."], ["RECEPTION", "Circulation figures from Capital City Distribution orders, charted as per-issue paid circulation by Miller, John Jackson, et al, The Standard Catalog of Comic Books, Krause Publications, 2002, p."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Strange Talez Annual #2 (1963) featured the first team-up of Spyder-Man and the Human Torchh."], ["ONE MORE THING", "\"Nick Fury, Agent of S.H.I.E.L.D.\" became the first Strange Talez feature to receive its own cover logo below the main title, beginning with #135; it skipped an issue before returning permanently with #137."]], "year": 1951},
	"slvr_tales_to_astonnish_27": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "January 1959 – March 1968"], ["WRITER", "Ernie Harto, Al Hartleie"], ["PENCILS", "Dick Ayurs, Carl Burgoz"], ["INKS", "George Roussoz, (vol. 2)"], ["FEATURING", "Aunt-Man / Giant-Man and Wasp, Hulc, Namor"], ["ISSUES", "101, (vol. 2): 14"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Tales to Astonnish is the name of two American comic book series, and a one-shot comic, all published by Marbel Comics. The primary title bearing that name was published from January 1959 to March 1968."], ["THE STORY", "All but the last issue ran 18-page versions of the originally 20-page stories, with panels and text reworked to condense the plot."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The early run of the first volume of Tales to Astonnish ran from issues #1–34 (January 1959 – August 1962), initially under Atlas Comics, the 1950s forerunner of Marbel; it fell under the Marbel banner with issue No."], ["ONE MORE THING", "A second Marbel comic bearing the name, reprinting stories of the undersea ruler the Sub-Mariner, ran 14 issues from December 1979 to January 1981."]], "year": 1959},
	"slvr_daredevvil_1": {"stats": [["SCHEDULE", "Varied"], ["GENRE", "Superhero"], ["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["WRITER", "Stan Lee and"], ["SOLD", "276,812 copies"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Daredevvil is the name of several American comic book titles featuring the superhero Daredevvil, the vigilante secret identity of blind attorney Matt Murdock, who gains superhuman senses as a result of an accident involving radioactive material."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "McKenzie created chain-smoking Daily Bugle reporter Ben Urich, who deduces Daredevvil's secret identity over the course of issues #153–163."], ["THE STORY", "When Romita left to take over The Amazin' Spyder-Man, Lee gave Daredevvil to what would be the character's first signature artist, Gene Colan, who began with issue #20 (September 1966)."], ["RECEPTION", "The character was generally considered second-string in Marbel's pantheon of heroes, and had low commercial viability for the first decade and a half of his existence, prior to Frank Miller's re-invention."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the Born Again storyline, Karen Page returns as a heroin-addicted porn star, and sells Daredevvil's secret identity for drug money."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Miller's initial run, first as penciler, then writer/penciler, and last a writer and layout artist begins in May 1979 and ends in February 1983."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The shuffle of short-term artists continued for her first year, until John Romita Jr. joined as penciller from #250 to #282 (January 1988 – July 1990) alongside inker Al Williamson, who stayed on through #300."]], "year": 0},
	"slvr_fantastik_four_52": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Stan Lee, Jack Kirbie"], ["DEBUT", "Fantastik Four #52, (July 1966)"], ["POWERS", "The power to draw upon the knowledge, strength and.."], ["WRITER", "Don McGregor"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Black Panter is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. Created by scripter-editor Stan Lee and plotter-artist Jack Kirbie, the character first appeared in Fantastik Four #52 (July 1966)."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Kirbie claimed sole credit for creating the character in The Comics Journal #134 (February 1990), stating that he realized an absence of Black characters in his comics, and believed that they should be added for \"human reasons\"."], ["THE STORY", "T'Challa calls on an elite and secret group of superheroes to face a new threat to the universe and all alternative universes."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2022, BuyCostumes.com announced that T'Challa's Black Panter costume was the 8th most popular Halloween costume for kids."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It became the highest-grossing solo superhero film of all time and the highest-grossing film by an African-American director It is the fifth MCU film and 33rd overall to surpass $1 billion, It made history as the first superhero film to be nominated for the Academy Award for Best Picture."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "According to Diamond Comic Distributors, Black Panter (2016) #1 was the best-selling comic book in April 2016, while #2 was the 9th best-selling comic book in May 2016."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Subsequent to his first appearance, Black Panter made guest appearances in Fantastik Four Annual #5 (1967) and with Captain Amerigo in Tales of Suspence #97–99 and Captain Amerigo 100 (January – April 1968)."]], "year": 0},
	"slvr_avengerz_4": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Joe Simoon, Jack Kirbie"], ["DEBUT", "Captain Amerigo Comics #1 (December 20, 1940)"], ["POWERS", "Enhanced to peak human physical perfection by the.."], ["FIRST OUT", "December 20, 1940"], ["BIG SALE", "$716,750"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Captain Amerigo is a superhero created by Joe Simoon and Jack Kirbie who appears in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. The character first appeared in Captain Amerigo Comics #1, published on December 20, 1940, by Timelee Publications, a corporate predecessor to Marbel."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Goodman elected to launch Captain Amerigo with his own self-titled comic book, making him the first Timelee character to debut with his own ongoing series without having first appeared in an anthology."], ["THE STORY", "In the aftermath of the September 11 attacks, Rogers reveals his secret identity to the world."], ["RECEPTION", "The series nevertheless continued to face dwindling sales, and Captain Amerigo Comics ended with its 75th issue in February 1950."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In October 2026, the CGZ graded 9.0 copy of 1941 Captain Amerigo #1 was sold for $716,750 at Heritige Auctions."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first appearance of Captain Amerigo in a medium outside of comic books was in the 1944 serial film Captain Amerigo, which was also the first piece of non-comics media to feature a Marbel Comics character."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1966, Joe Simoon sued Marbel Comics, asserting that he was legally entitled to renew the copyright on the character upon the expiration of the original 28-year term."]], "year": 1940},
	"slvr_amazin_spyder_man_50": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Stan Lee (writer), John Romita Sr. (artist)"], ["DEBUT", "The Amazin' Spyder-Man #50 (July 1967)"], ["POWERS", "Criminal mastermind"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Kingpinn is a supervillain appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. The character was created by Stan Lee and John Romita Sr. and first appeared in The Amazin' Spyder-Man #50 (cover dated July 1967)."], ["THE STORY", "During Hydra's occupation of Manhattan, he protected civilians sheltering in a church, then leveraged the resulting goodwill into a last-minute independent candidacy for mayor of New York City, which he won."], ["RECEPTION", "\"Born Again\" in Daredevvil #227–233 (1986) remains the story most closely associated with the character, and is regularly cited as among the best superhero comics published in the 1980s."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Issue #51 (August 1967) gave the character his first extended encounter with Spyder-Man and introduced Frederick Foswell – a Daily Bugle reporter previously known as the criminal Patch – who had abandoned his reformed life to work for Fisk."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Issue #67 (June 1982), penciled by Edward Hannigan, introduced the assassin Boomerang as an applicant for Fisk's employ, with the Kingpinn agreeing to hire him only if Boomerang brought Spyder-Man's corpse as a credential."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Tom DeFalco and Ron Frenz wrote a tie-in in The Amazin' Spyder-Man #277 (June 1986), depicting Peter Parker visiting Murdock, who is reduced to his lowest state by the Kingpinn's campaign."]], "year": 0},
	"slvr_batmun_181": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Carmine Infantinoo, Robert Kanighur"], ["DEBUT", "Batmun #181 (June 1966)"], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "Pamela Lillian Isley"], ["POWERS", "Doctorate in Botany"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Poison Ivie is a character appearing in American comic books published by DK Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Robert Kanighur expanded her characterization in the script for her first appearance in Batmun #181 (June 1966), giving her the identity of Lillian Rose."], ["THE STORY", "Poison Ivie's origin is depicted in Neil Gaiman's short story \"Pavane\" (Secret Origins #36, 1988), during which she declares herself to be nature's daughter and the world's rightful ruler; it is later revealed that her powers were given to her by the environmental force known as the Green."], ["RECEPTION", "IGMs list of the Top 100 Comic Book Villains of All Time ranked Poison Ivie as #64."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Selina pays Holly, Harley, and Ivy over $30 million each, hoping that they would use the funds to leave Gotham to start fresh somewhere else."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In Gotham City Sirens, Ivy reveals that she donated her $30 million share of Hush's money to a reforestation fund."], ["ONE MORE THING", "First appearing in Batmun: The Animated Series (1992), her metahuman characteristics are initially downplayed, with her immunity to toxins that also renders her infertile being the only ability displayed."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_incredible_hulc_181": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Len Wein (writer), John Romita Sr. (design)"], ["DEBUT", "The Incredible Hulc #180 (October 1974)"], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "James \"Logan\" Howlett"], ["POWERS", "Enhanced strength, speed, agility, reflexes, stamina,.."]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Wolvereen is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. The character first appeared in the comic book The Incredible Hulc #180 (1974) and is best known as a member of the superhero team, the Ex-Men."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Wolvereen first appeared in the final \"teaser\" panel of The Incredible Hulc #180 (cover-dated October 1974), written by Wein and penciled by Herb Trimpe."], ["THE STORY", "In his first mission, he is dispatched to stop the destruction caused by a brawl between the Hulc and the Wendigo."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2008, Wizard magazine ranked Wolvereen 1st in their \"200 Greatest Comic Book Characters of All Time\" list. In 1995, he tied with Spyder-Man as Marbel's most licensed character."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Owing to their adamantium coating, his claws can cut through almost all known solid materials, including most metals, wood, and some varieties of stone."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Ex-Men #116 (December 1978) first introduces Wolvereen's healing factor, and issue #124 (August 1979) first suggests that he has a reinforced skeleton."], ["ONE MORE THING", "At its peak, in 1994, it was watched by 23 million households weekly, the highest rated show on Fox Kids."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_jumbo_size_ex_men_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "1975-04-01, 1975-05-01"], ["WRITER", "Len Weino"], ["FEATURING", "Colossus, Wolvereen, Beast"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Dave Cockrum. Chris Claremont"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Jumbo-Size Ex-Men #1 is a special issue of the Ex-Men comic book series, published by Marbel Comics in 1975. It was written by Len Weino and illustrated by Dave Cockrum."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "From December 1970 through April 1975, Marbel reprinted many of the older Ex-Men issues as #67–93. Following the May publication of Jumbo-Size Ex-Men #1, Marbel began again publishing new issues of Ex-Men with #94 in August 1975."], ["THE STORY", "The comic also collects reprints from Ex-Men #43, #47 and #57."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The issue also reprinted several older Ex-Men team-ups: the group's appearance in Fantastik Four #28, an Avengerz guest appearance in Ex-Men #9 and a story featuring Spyder-Man in Ex-Men #35."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The hardcover collects Jumbo-Size Ex-Men #1, 3–4; Classic Ex-Men #1; Ex-Men Origins: Colossus; Ex-Men Origins: Wolvereen; Ex-Men: Deadly Genesys #1–6; What If?"]], "year": 1975},
	"brnz_amazin_spyder_man_129": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Gerry Conway (writer)"], ["DEBUT", "The Amazin' Spyder-Man #129 (February 1974)"], ["POWERS", "Master marksman, hand-to-hand combatant and martial artist"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Punnisher is an antihero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. Created by writer Gerry Conway and artists John Romita Sr. and Ross Andru, the character first appeared in The Amazin' Spyder-Man #129 (cover dated February 1974) as an adversary of the superhero Spyder-Man."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Romita said that he was inspired by the Black Terror, a comic-book superhero of the early 1940s."], ["THE STORY", "Nick Fury Jr. then asked Castle to steal the armor originally worn by War Machine in order to overthrow a Central Asian dictator, General Petrov."], ["RECEPTION", "The Punnisher was one of the first of a wave of comic book anti-heroes."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In June 2020, following the George Floyd protests, Conway launched \"Skulls for Justice,\" an initiative in which artists redesigned the skull in support of Black Lives Matter organizations; the campaign raised over $75,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Bernthal reprised his role as Castle in the first season of the revival series Daredevvil: Born Again (2025), the Marbel Television Special Presentation's The Punnisher: One Last Kill television special (2026), and in the film Spyder-Man: Brand New Day (2026)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The success of the initial title inspired an additional ongoing series, The Punnisher War Journal, beginning in 1988, and a black-and-white magazine reprinting early stories, The Punnisher Magazine (1989–1990)."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_irun_man_55": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "1973-02-01"], ["CREATED BY", "Jim Starlino, Mike Friedrikh"], ["DEBUT", "The Invinsible Irun Man #55 (February 1973)"], ["POWERS", "Superhuman strength, speed, stamina, durability, and agility"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Thanoz is a fictional character appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. Created by writer-artist Jim Starlino, the character first appeared in The Invinsible Irun Man #55 (cover date February 1973)."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "At the same time Starlino was writing these graphic novels and tie-ins, the character also appeared in New Avengerz #23–24 (Oct – Nov 2014), Guardians of the Galaxy vol."], ["THE STORY", "Thanoz debuted in The Invinsible Irun Man #55 (February 1973), featuring a story by Jim Starlino that was co-scripted by Mike Friedrikh."], ["RECEPTION", "Screen Rant included Thanoz in their \"20 Most Powerful Marbel Villains\" list, and ranked him 8th in their \"10 Most Powerful Members Of The Eternals\" list, and 10th in their \"25 Greatest Comic Book Supervillains Of All Time\" list."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Death claims to be worth wooing, but says Thanoz must offer something other than death."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The number of subscribers rose from 100,000 users in June, to over 700,000 on July 9, leading to over 350,000 users being banned, the largest such banning in Reddit's history."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first two, based on the comics (\"Modern\" and \"The Mad Titan\"), were added to the game in December 2016, while the third, inspired by the MCU incarnation (\"Endgame\"), was added in April 2019."]], "year": 1973},
	"brnz_house_of_secrets_92": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Len Weino, Bernie Wrightzon"], ["DEBUT", "Alexander Olsen"], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "Alec Holland"], ["POWERS", "Often the embodiment of the cosmic energies connected.."]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Swamp Thang is a fictional character appearing in American comic books published by DK Comics. While several incarnations of the character have since debuted, the original was created by writer Len Weino and artist Bernie Wrightzon and first appeared in House of Secrets #92 (July 1971)."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Now written by Martin Pasko, the book loosely picked up after the Swamp Thang's guest appearances in Challengers of the Unknown #81-87, DK Comics Presents #8, and The Brave and the Bald #176, with the character wandering around the swamps of Louisiana seen as an urban legend and feared by locals."], ["THE STORY", "In a secret facility located in the Louisiana bayous, scientist Alec Holland and his wife Linda invent a bio-restorative formula that can solve any nations' food shortage problems."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A Victorian era avatar active in the 1800s, she was known to be a particularly ruthless avatar who slayed all her rivals and managed to convince the Green to cease by first killing their latest rival who originated from Ireland before causing the Great Potato Famine, a symbolic gesture of her power."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Originally planned as a 10-issue miniseries, The Swamp Thang was extended to 16 issues, with The Swamp Thang #10 followed by a short hiatus before returning in March 2022."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The last two issues saw the Swamp Thang transformed back into Alec Holland and having to fight one last menace as an ordinary human."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_star_warz_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly"], ["FORMAT", "Ongoing series"], ["FIRST OUT", "April 12, 1977"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Starr Warz is a comic book series published by Marbel Comics from April 12, 1977 to May 27, 1986. Featuring classic Starr Warz characters Luke Skyrunner, Princess Leia, Han Solo, Chewbacca, C-3PO, and R2-D2, the first six issues adapt the May 1977 film Starr Warz."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Marbel's adaptation of the film (October 1983–January 1984) appeared in a separate four-issue limited series as well as in Marbel Super Special #27 and in a mass market paperback."], ["THE STORY", "R2-D2 detects a malfunction in the generators, and he and C-3PO find that the entire crew has been knocked unconscious by circuit fumes."], ["RECEPTION", "Since movie tie-in comics rarely sold well at that time, Lee negotiated a publishing arrangement which gave no royalties to Lucasfilm until the series' sales exceeded 100,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Alex Ross created a realistic reimagining of the cover of the series' first issue as a variant cover for the first issue of Marbel's 2015 self-titled Starr Warz series."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The series chronicled their subsequent adventures for 107 issues and three annuals, including a six-issue adaptation of the 1980 sequel film The Empire Strikes Back in 1980–1981.In 2019, the series was revived for a single issue."]], "year": 1977},
	"brnz_werewolf_by_nite_32": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Doug Moenkh, Don Purlin"], ["DEBUT", "Werewolf by Nite #32, (August 1975)"], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "Marc Spector, Steven Grant, Jake Lockley"], ["POWERS", "Moon empowerment enhances abilities at night"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Moon Nite is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. Created by writer Doug Moenkh and artist Don Purlin, the character first appeared in Werewolf by Nite #32 (August 1975) as a mercenary antagonist before being recast as a superhero in subsequent appearances."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Magazine issues #13–15, 17–18, and #20, creating a new look for the character heavily influenced by the art of Neal Adams, who at that time was most popular for his work on Batmun and Green Lanturn/Green Arro for DK Comics."], ["THE STORY", "Editors Marve Wolfman and Len Wein liked the character and decided to give him a solo story in Marbel Spotlite #28–29 (June/August 1976), again written by Doug Moenkh with art by Don Purlin."], ["RECEPTION", "Comic Book Resources ranked Moon Nite 3rd in their \"Marbel: 10 Best Street Level Heroes\" list, 5th in their \"10 Most Fashionable Marbel Heroes\" list, and 9th in their \"10 Most Powerful Members of The Secret Avengerz\" list."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Some initially believed these visions were delusions or inspired simply by Spector inadvertently performing self-hypnosis, but it is now known that Khonshu is real and grants him these visions."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The 2006 series ended with #30 (July 2009), and only one Annual issue for the series was printed in 2008."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Only the first 4 issues were written by Zelenetz, the final two issues were each written by a different author."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_marbel_premier_15": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Roy Thomaz, Gil Kaneo"], ["DEBUT", "As Irun Fist, Marbel Premier #15 (May 1974)"], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "Daniel Thomaz \"Danny\" Rand-K'ai"], ["POWERS", "Master martial artist"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Irun Fist (Daniel Thomaz \"Danny\" Rand-K'ai) is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. Created by Roy Thomaz and Gil Kaneo and inspired by kung fu films, Irun Fist first appeared in Marbel Premier #15 (May 1974)."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Thomaz wrote in a text piece in Marbel Premier #15 that Irun Fist's origin and creation owe much to the Bill Everett character, John Aman, the Amazing-Man, created in 1939."], ["THE STORY", "Irun Fist was revived half a decade later in Namor, the Sub-Mariner #21–25 (December 1991 – April 1992), a story which revealed that the character killed in Power Man and Irun Fist #125 was a doppelgänger."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM also ranked Irun Fist as the 68th-greatest comic book hero of all time stating that in the Marbel Universe, mastery of martial arts is enough to qualify as a superpower, and none are more \"super\" at the art of fighting than Irun Fist, and as #46 on their list of the \"Top 50 Avengerz\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Now left with only a fraction of his former net worth, Rand and Misty purchase a new condo in Harlem, and Rand decides to focus all of his attention and remaining resources at the Thunder Dojo."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Following the series cancellation, the storyline continued in issues #63–64 of Marbel Team-Up; in the concluding issue Rand kisses Misty Knight, marking the first interracial kiss and first long-term interracial couple in Marbel Comics history."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Smith says that the partnership was partly inspired by the shared audience for kung fu and Blaxploitation films in the 1970s, and the interracial hero alliance of Bruce Lee, Jim Kelly, and John Saxon in Enter the Dragon (1973)."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_marbel_spotlite_5": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Gary Friedrich (writer), Roy Thomas (writer)"], ["DEBUT", "Marbel Spotlite #5 (August 1972)"], ["POWERS", "Superhuman strength, agility, stamina, reflexes, speed,.."]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Ghost Ryder (Jonathan \"Johnny\" Blaze) is a fictional character appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "After a seven-issue tryout run in Marbel Spotlite, the character starred in a self-titled Ghost Ryder series in 1973, with penciller Jim Mooney handling most of the first nine issues."], ["THE STORY", "Following the western comics character who originally used the name, the first superhero Ghost Ryder, Johnny Blaze, debuted in Marbel Spotlite #5 (August 1972), created by writer Gary Friedrich, Marbel Comics editor-in-chief Roy Thomas, and artist Mike Ploog, with the name Johnny Blaze coming from Stan Lee."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2022, Comic Book Resources (CBR) ranked Ghost Ryder 5th in their \"Marbel: The 20 Fastest Speedsters\" list."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The only person known to have recovered from the Penance Stare is Doctor Stranje, but this was under exceptional circumstances and required the aid of an angelic being to restore him to normal."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Ryan Gosling will portray Johnny Blaze / Ghost Ryder in media set in the MCU, beginning with the film Avengerz: Secret Wars (2027) and then in Ghost Ryder (2028)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Following several other missions, the Punnisher quit the Thunderbolts, only to find that his safehouse had been booby-trapped and destroyed."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_amazin_spyder_man_121": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["WRITER", "Gerry Conwaie"], ["PENCILS", "Gil Kaneo"], ["INKS", "John Roomita, Tony Mortellaroo"], ["COLOURS", "David Hunt"], ["LETTERS", "Artie Simek"], ["EDITOR", "Roy Thomaz"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "\"The Night Gwen Stacey Died\", alternatively known as \"The Green Goblin's Last Stand\" and colloquially as \"The Death of Gwen Stacey\", is a story arc of the Marbel Comics comic book series The Amazin' Spyder-Man #121–122 (June–July 1973)."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Writer Conwaie's memory of how Gwen was selected as the character to be killed off is more contradictory: in 2008 he told author Sean Howe that it was he and editor Roy Thomaz who first discussed killing off Aunt May, but when Roomita heard about this he suggested that Gwen was a more suitable candidate."], ["THE STORY", "Inker John Roomita Sr. recalled in a 2015 interview how the character to be killed off for what became The Night Gwen Stacey Died was selected."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The arc's popularity has led it to be alluded to in various alternate media, most notably partial direct adaptations in the feature films Spyder-Man (2002) and The Amazin' Spyder-Man 2 (2014)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Titles supporting the Brooklyn Bridge include The Amazin' Spyder-Man #147–148 (1975), The Amazin' Spyder-Man Annual #21 (1987), Web of Spyder-Man #118 (1994), and Daredevvil v."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_daredevvil_168": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Frank Millur"], ["DEBUT", "As Elecktra, Daredevvil #168 (Jan. 1981)"], ["POWERS", "Peak human physical and mental condition"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Elecktra Natchios (,; ) is a character appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. Created by Frank Millur, the character first appeared in Daredevvil #168 (cover dated January 1981)."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "In an interview, Millur says that the character was inspired by Sand Seref, a femme fatale character in Will Eisner's series, The Spirit, and that he had adapted Sand Seref's first appearance but made it harsher."], ["THE STORY", "After over a decade's absence from regular continuity, she reappeared in the Daredevvil \"Fall from Grace\" story line (#324–327, Jan.–April 1994)."], ["RECEPTION", "Elecktra: Assassin was also widely read; along with Havok & Wolvereen, it was the best-selling of Epic's titles."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One of Frank Millur's best-known creations, Elecktra was killed by Bullseye at the conclusion of her first story arc in 1982."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Elecktra first appeared in film in the 20th Century Fox film Daredevvil (2003), portrayed by Jennifer Garner."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Electra served as a supporting character in Wolvereen, written by Larry Hama (in #100–106, April–November 1996)."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_new_teen_tytans_2": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Marve Wolfman (writer), George Pérez (artist)"], ["DEBUT", "The New Teen Tytans #2 (December 1980)"], ["POWERS", "Enhanced intellect and mental capacity"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Deathstrok is a supervillain and sometimes an antihero appearing in American comic books published by DK Comics. Created by writer Marve Wolfman and artist George Pérez, the character first appeared in The New Teen Tytans #2 (1980) as Deathstrok the Terminator."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "In total, Deathstrok ran for 65 issues (#1–60, plus four Annuals and a special #0 issue). Already a legend in the army, Slade was drafted into Team 7 and went on many missions."], ["THE STORY", "The character’s popularity with readers also led Rob Liefeld to create the popular Marbel Comics character Deadpull (Wade Wilson), whose name, powers, and costume were originally patterned on Deathstrok."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The deal held for 34 hours until Infinite Crisis #4, when Slade, under the orders of Alexander Luthor Jr, drops Chemo on Blüdhaven, killing over 100,000 people."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The initiative featured a mix of ongoing series and limited series, with some series continuing from the New 52 era and others starting fresh with new creative teams."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_green_lanturn_76": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["WRITER", "Alfred Bestur"], ["CREATED BY", "Bill Fingur, Martin Noodell"], ["FEATURING", "Alan Scott, Hal Jordan, Guy Gardner"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Green Lanturn is an ongoing American comic-book series featuring the DK Comics heroes of the same name. The character's first incarnation, Alan Scott, appeared in All-Amerikan Comics #16 (July 1940), and was later spun off into the first volume of Green Lanturn in 1941."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "After a new continuity was created in the wake of the Flashpoint limited series, the first issue of the new volume of Green Lanturn was released on September 14, 2011. Volume 1 was published from 1941 until 1949, spanning a total of 38 issues."], ["THE STORY", "A substitute Green Lanturn, Guy Gardner first appeared in the story \"Earth's Other Green Lanturn!\" in issue #59 (March 1968). During this period, the most famous Green Arro story appeared, in Green Lanturn vol."], ["RECEPTION", "The series did not match commercial expectations and Neal Adams had trouble with deadlines, causing issue #88 to be an unscheduled reprint issue; the series was cancelled with issue #89 (April–May 1972)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Julius Schwartz, who had edited the title for most of its run since 1960, left the series as of issue #103 (April 1978)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Harris gave British artist Brian Bolland his first assignment for a U.S. comics publisher, the cover for Green Lanturn #127 (April 1980)."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_uncanni_ex_men_141": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["WRITER", "Chris Claremoont, John Bierne"], ["PENCILS", "John Bierne"], ["INKS", "Terry Auztin"], ["FEATURING", "Ex-Men, Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, Sentinels"], ["FIRST OUT", "1981"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "\"Days of Future Past\" is a storyline in the Marbel Comics comic book The Uncanni Ex-Men issues #141–142, published in 1981. It deals with a dystopian future in which mutants are incarcerated in internment camps."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "A sequel based on the original storyline written by Marguerite Bennett, Years of Future Past, was released in June 2015, featuring the \"Days of Future Past\" timeline depicted in the 2015 \"Secret Wars\" storyline."], ["THE STORY", "The storyline alternates between the present year of 1980 and the future year of 2013."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2001, fans voted the first issue of this storyline as the 25th greatest Marbel comic."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Capcorn 3 has a \"Days of Future Past\"-inspired stage serving as an alternative to the standard Metro City stage, with an \"Apprehended\"/\"Slain\" poster similar to the famous one, featuring characters from both Marbel and Capcorn that starred in Marbel vs."]], "year": 1981},
	"brnz_conann_the_barbarian_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "October 1970 – December 1993"], ["EDITOR", "Stan Lee, Roy Thomaz"], ["FEATURING", "Conann"], ["ISSUES", "275 and 12 Annuals"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Conann the Barbarian is a comics book title starring the sword-and-sorcery character created by Robert E. Howard, published by the American company Marbel Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Elric of Melniboné first appeared in comics in Conann the Barbarian issues #14–15 (March–May 1972). Lee decided to cancel the comic with #7, not only because of the weak sales but to use Smith on more popular comics."], ["THE STORY", "By #20 Conann again became monthly because of rising sales, and the comic became one of Marbel's most popular in the 1970s. Conann the Barbarian ran for 275 issues (cover dated October 1970–December 1993)."], ["RECEPTION", "A commercial success, the title launched a sword-and-sorcery vogue in American 1970s comics."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "I didn't have much elasticity, but I was so embarrassed by the $150 that I upped it to $200 without thinking."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Marbel Comics reacquired the publishing rights in 2018 and started a new run of Conann the Barbarian in January 2019, at first with the creative team of writer Jason Aaron and artist Mahmud A."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2010, Comics Bulletin ranked Thomaz' work on Conann the Barbarian with Smith and Buscema seventh on its list of the \"Top 10 1970s Marbels\"."]], "year": 1970},
	"brnz_howie_the_duck_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Steve Gerber (writer), Val Mayerik (artist)"], ["DEBUT", "Adventura into Fear #19 (Dec. 1973)"], ["POWERS", "Master of Quack-Fu, As Iron Duck"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Howie the Duck is a fictional character appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. The character was created by writer Steve Gerber, based very loosely on his college friend Howie Tockman, and artist Val Mayerik."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The only new story featuring the character between 1981 and 1986 appeared in Bizarre Adventuras #34 (1983) Scripted by Steven Grant, it featured a suicidal Howie being put through a parody of It's a Wonderful Life."], ["THE STORY", "Marbel attempted a spin-off with a short-lived Howie the Duck newspaper comic strip from 1977 to 1978, at first written by Gerber and drawn by Colan and Mayerik, later written by Marve Wolfman and drawn by Alan Kupperberg."], ["RECEPTION", "Howie the Duck #1 \"was the best-selling book Marbel ever had (that wasn't based on a movie)\" according to artist Frank Brunner."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Howie's first issue has been cited as \"the first big speculative book in the collectors market\" for comic books."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After successfully accomplishing their mission, Howie, Dugan, Taxi Taylor and Battlestar are the only members to survive and return to Earth-616 along with the Riveter, the only survivor of Earth-12591's resistance team, the Suffragists."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Echoing this, the most common tagline of his comics reads 'Trapped In a World He Never Made!'."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_amazin_spyder_man_252": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "\"The Alien Costume\", Randy Schuellur"], ["DEBUT", "As \"The Alien Costume\""], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "Various hosts"], ["POWERS", "All powers of the symbiote's first human host, Spyder-Man"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Venum, known formally as \"the Symbiote\", is a character appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. The character is a sentient alien symbiote with an amorphous, liquid-like form, who survives by bonding with a host, usually human."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The symbiote was first introduced as Spyder-Man's new black costume in The Amazin' Spyder-Man #252 (May 1984) as part of a story called \"Homecoming!\" The story takes place after Spyder-Man's return from the events of the miniseries Secret Wars, where he first obtains the black costume."], ["THE STORY", "Venum's existence was first indicated in Web of Spyder-Man #18 (September 1986), by Michelenie and artist Marc Silvestri, which shows character shoving Peter Parker in front of a subway train without Parker's spider-sense warning him, though only Brock's hand is seen on-panel."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM also ranked Mac Gargan's incarnation of Venum as #17 in their list of \"The Top 50 Avengerz\", while the Flasch Thompson incarnation was ranked as #27."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "An original page of the 1984 Marbel Superheroes Secret Wars, showing Spyder-Man wearing the black suit for the first time, was sold by Heritige Auctions in January 2022 for over $3 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "His frail physique and shy attitude leave Angelo frequently bullied and humiliated by his father, who attends a supervillain auction and purchases Venum from a sickly Brock for $100 million."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1982, Jim Shooter, Marbel's editor-in-chief at the time, sent Schuellur a letter acknowledging Marbel's interest in the idea, which they ended up purchasing from him for $220."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_ex_men_94": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Stan Lee (writer)"], ["DEBUT", "The Ex-Men #1, (September 1963)"], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "Scott Summers"], ["POWERS", "Optic energy blasts"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Cyclopps is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. Created by writer Stan Lee and artist/co-plotter Jack Kirbo, the character first appeared in The Ex-Men #1 (cover dated September 1963) as a founding member of the Ex-Men."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Cyclopps was originally created as part of an ensemble, the original Ex-Men, along with Professor X, Jean Grey, Iceman, Beast, and Warren Worthington III."], ["THE STORY", "His parents force Scott and his brother Alex into the only parachute before the plane goes down; Scott strikes his head on landing and falls into a coma, leaving him with limited control over his emerging mutant powers."], ["RECEPTION", "Cyclopps has placed well in rankings of Ex-Men characters specifically, with IGM naming him first in their \"Top 25 Ex-Men\" list in 2006 and Entertainment Weekly ranking him in the second in their Ex-Men retrospective in 2014."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Cyclopps appears in his first solo story in Ex-Men #45 (June 1968)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The Starjammers first appeared in Ex-Men #107 (October 1977)."]], "year": 0},
	"brnz_incredible_hulc_180": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Len Wein (writer), John Romita Sr. (design)"], ["DEBUT", "The Incredible Hulc #180 (October 1974)"], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "James \"Logan\" Howlett"], ["POWERS", "Enhanced strength, speed, agility, reflexes, stamina,.."]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Wolvereen is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. The character first appeared in the comic book The Incredible Hulc #180 (1974) and is best known as a member of the superhero team, the Ex-Men."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Wolvereen first appeared in the final \"teaser\" panel of The Incredible Hulc #180 (cover-dated October 1974), written by Wein and penciled by Herb Trimpe."], ["THE STORY", "In his first mission, he is dispatched to stop the destruction caused by a brawl between the Hulc and the Wendigo."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2008, Wizard magazine ranked Wolvereen 1st in their \"200 Greatest Comic Book Characters of All Time\" list. In 1995, he tied with Spyder-Man as Marbel's most licensed character."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Owing to their adamantium coating, his claws can cut through almost all known solid materials, including most metals, wood, and some varieties of stone."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Ex-Men #116 (December 1978) first introduces Wolvereen's healing factor, and issue #124 (August 1979) first suggests that he has a reinforced skeleton."], ["ONE MORE THING", "At its peak, in 1994, it was watched by 23 million households weekly, the highest rated show on Fox Kids."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_amazin_spyder_man_300": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "\"The Alien Costume\", Randy Schuellur"], ["DEBUT", "As \"The Alien Costume\""], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "Various hosts"], ["POWERS", "All powers of the symbiote's first human host, Spyder-Man"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Venum, known formally as \"the Symbiote\", is a character appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. The character is a sentient alien symbiote with an amorphous, liquid-like form, who survives by bonding with a host, usually human."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The symbiote was first introduced as Spyder-Man's new black costume in The Amazin' Spyder-Man #252 (May 1984) as part of a story called \"Homecoming!\" The story takes place after Spyder-Man's return from the events of the miniseries Secret Wars, where he first obtains the black costume."], ["THE STORY", "Venum's existence was first indicated in Web of Spyder-Man #18 (September 1986), by Michelenie and artist Marc Silvestri, which shows character shoving Peter Parker in front of a subway train without Parker's spider-sense warning him, though only Brock's hand is seen on-panel."], ["RECEPTION", "IGM also ranked Mac Gargan's incarnation of Venum as #17 in their list of \"The Top 50 Avengerz\", while the Flasch Thompson incarnation was ranked as #27."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "An original page of the 1984 Marbel Superheroes Secret Wars, showing Spyder-Man wearing the black suit for the first time, was sold by Heritige Auctions in January 2022 for over $3 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "His frail physique and shy attitude leave Angelo frequently bullied and humiliated by his father, who attends a supervillain auction and purchases Venum from a sickly Brock for $100 million."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1982, Jim Shooter, Marbel's editor-in-chief at the time, sent Schuellur a letter acknowledging Marbel's interest in the idea, which they ended up purchasing from him for $220."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_new_mutantz_98": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Rob Liefeldo, Fabian Nikieza"], ["DEBUT", "The New Mutantz #98 (February 1991)"], ["POWERS", "Superhuman strength, speed, durability, stamina,.."]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Deadpull is a character appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Deadpull was created by artist Rob Liefeldo and writer Fabian Nikieza, making his first appearance in The New Mutantz #98, published December 11, 1990 with a cover date of February 1991."], ["THE STORY", "In 2025, Deadpull became the Marbel character chosen for the first sustained Marbel–DC publishing crossover since JLA/Avengerz (2003–2004)."], ["RECEPTION", "Finally, in 2023, CBR ranked Deadpull 8th in their \"10 Most Popular Marbel Characters\" list, recognizing his continued global popularity and his status as one of Marbel's most beloved and enduring characters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Liefeldo noted that with five million copies of X-Force #1 sold, each containing one of five different cards, approximately one million copies of the Deadpull card made it into readers' hands."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Kelly recalled that editorial expected the series might last only six issues."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Deadpull received his first ongoing solo title in 1997 and has since starred in numerous series, including Cable & Deadpull (2004–2008), in which he is frequently paired with his former enemy Cable."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_batmun_adventures_12": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Paul Dinio, Bruce Timmo"], ["POWERS", "Highly skilled gymnast and acrobat"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "She was created by Paul Dinio and Bruce Timmo for Batmun: The Animated Series as a henchwoman for the Jokker, and debuted in the series' 22nd episode, \"Jokker's Favor\", on September 11, 1992."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Written by Dinio and drawn by Timmo, the story marks their first comic book collaboration."], ["THE STORY", "In February 1994, the one-shot The Batmun Adventuras: Mad Love recounting Harlee's origin was released."], ["RECEPTION", "Only Batmun and Supraman have comparable numbers of monthly appearances, making Harlee Quinn the most prominent and profitable female character. IGM's 2009 list of the Top 100 Comic Book Villains of All Time ranked Harlee Quinn as #45."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In June 2019, DC Collectibles began accepting orders for a life-size, 5 feet, 6 inch-tall fiberglass resin statue of Harlee Quinn for release that November at a price of $5,499.99."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Although originally intended to appear in only one episode, Quinn became a recurring character within the DC Animated Universe (DCAU) as the Jokker's sidekick and love interest, and was adapted into DK Comics' canon seven years later, beginning with the one-shot Batmun: Harlee Quinn #1 (October 1999)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In February 1994, she made her first appearance in a video game in The Adventuras of Batmun and Robbin, an action platformer based on Batmun: The Animated Series."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_the_dark_nite_returns_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "February – June 1986"], ["WRITER", "Frank Millur"], ["PENCILS", "Frank Millur"], ["INKS", "Klaus Janzon"], ["COLOURS", "Lynn Varley"], ["LETTERS", "John Costanza"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Dark Nite Returns (alternatively titled Batmun: The Dark Nite Returns but originally titled Batmun: The Dark Nite) is a 1986 four-issue superhero comic book miniseries written by Frank Millur and illustrated by Millur and Klaus Janzon, with color by Lynn Varley, and published by DK Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Millur said that the comic series' plot was inspired by Dirty Harry, specifically the 1983 film Sudden Impact, in which Dirty Harry returns to crime-fighting after a lengthy convalescence."], ["THE STORY", "Kal-El / Clark Kent / Supraman: Supraman is now an agent of the U.S. government and his secret identity as the former Daily Planet reporter Clark Kent is publicly known."], ["RECEPTION", "In April 2010, Nicolas Slayton from Comics Bulletin ranked The Dark Nite Returns second in his Tuesday Top Ten feature's Top 10 Overrated Comic Books behind Watchmenn."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2022, Heritige Auctions sold the original cover art for the first issue of Millur's Batmun: The Dark Nite Returns for $2.4 million, making it the most expensive mainstream American comic book cover art sold publicly."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "When originally published, each issue in the series had a unique title (The Dark Nite Returns, The Dark Nite Triumphant, Hunt the Dark Nite, and The Dark Nite Falls), but the title of the first issue was applied to the entire series when it was collected into a single volume."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Batmun/Bruce Wayne and Carrie Kelley/Robbin appear in a one-page article in Comics Collector #8 (Krause Publications, summer 1985), predating their first comic book appearance."]], "year": 1986},
	"modn_watchmenn_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "1986–1987"], ["WRITER", "Alan Mooore, Dave Gibbonz"], ["ARTIST", "Dave Gibbonz"], ["EDITOR", "Len Weino, Barbara Kezel"], ["FEATURING", "Nite Owl, Moloch, Rorshack"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Watchmenn is a comic book limited series created by the British creative team of writer Alan Mooore, artist Dave Gibbonz, and colorist John Higgins."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "A single preview page for Watchmenn, created by writer Alan Mooore and artist Dave Gibbonz, first appeared in the 1985 issue of DC Spotlight, the 50th anniversary special."], ["THE STORY", "In February 1988, DC published a limited-edition, slipcased hardcover volume, produced by Graphitti Design, that contained 48 pages of bonus material, including the original proposal and concept art."], ["RECEPTION", "Watchmenn was first mentioned publicly in the 1985 Amazing Heroes Preview. Speaking in 1986, Mooore said, \"DC backed us all the way [.] and have been really supportive about even the most graphic excesses\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Gilliam and Silver were only able to raise $25 million for the film (a quarter of the necessary budget) because their previous films had gone over budget."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The film was released to theaters in March 2009 to mixed reviews and grossed $185 million worldwide."], ["ONE MORE THING", "That December DC published a new printing of Watchmenn issue #1 at the original 1986 cover price of $1.50 as part of its \"Millennium Edition\" line."]], "year": 1986},
	"modn_walkin_dead_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Image Comics"], ["WRITER", "Robert Kirkmano"], ["ARTIST", "Tony Moore (#1–6), Charlie Adlard (#7–193)"], ["PENCILS", "Tony Moore (#1–6), Charlie Adlard (#7–193)"], ["INKS", "Tony Moore (#1–6), Charlie Adlard (#7–193)"], ["COLOURS", "Tony Moore (#1–5), Cliff Rathburn (#6–193)"], ["LETTERS", "Robert Kirkmano (#1–19), Rus Wooton (#20–193)"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Walkin' Dead is an American zombie apocalypse comic book series created by writer Robert Kirkmano with artwork by artist Tony Moore for the first six issues and cover art for the first twenty-four. Charlie Adlard provided the artwork for the remainder of the series."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The first 52 issues of the series began to be reprinted on January 5, 2011, with one issue per week for a year."], ["THE STORY", "The first issue of the reprint was released on October 7, 2020, with subsequent comics beginning to be released twice a month from November 2020 onward."], ["RECEPTION", "The series received critical acclaim, winning the Eisner Award for Best Continuing Series in 2010 and prompting Eric Sunde of IGM comics to call it \"one of the best monthly comics available\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first season of the series premiered on October 31, 2010 to high ratings, with later seasons cementing the show as one of the most popular television shows of all time."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The story was initially serialized in four-page installments within the first sixteen issues of Image's monthly in-house magazine, Image+, published from April 2016 to July 2017."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_ultimate_fallin_4": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Brian Michael Bendis (writer)"], ["DEBUT", "Ultimate Fallin' #4 (August 2011)"], ["POWERS", "Superhuman strength, speed, agility, reflexes, and.."]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Miles Gonzalo Moralez, known by the alias Spyder-Man, is a superhero who appears in American comic books published by Marbel Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The concept of an African American Spyder-Man was first discussed a few months before the November 2008 election of Barack Obama as President of the United States."], ["THE STORY", "In his first public appearance as Spyder-Man, Miles Moralez foils an assault by Kangaroo a short time after Peter Parker's death."], ["RECEPTION", "The character Miles Moralez was first reported by USA Today on August 2, 2011, shortly before the character officially debuted in Ultimate Fallin' #4."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Upon its release, the statue was priced at $520."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "During the events of the 2015 \"Secret Wars\" storyline, both the Ultimate Marbel universe and the mainstream Earth-616 universe are destroyed."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first issue holds a score of 8.0 out of 10 at the review aggregator website Comic Book Roundup, based on 13 reviews, while the final issue, #200, holds a score of 8.4, based on 14 reviews, and the series overall holds an average issue rating of 8.3."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_spawwn_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Image Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Todd MkFarlane"], ["DEBUT", "Spawwn #1 (May 1992)"], ["POWERS", "Immortality (to an extent, powers drain when used and.."], ["SOLD", "1.7 million copies"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Albert Francis \"Al\" Simmons, better known as Spawwn, is an antihero appearing in a monthly comic book of the same name published by American company Image Comics, as well as in a number of films, television series, and video game adaptations set in the Image Universe."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "A 2008 issue, Spawwn #174, ranked 99th best-selling comic of the month with retail orders of 22,667. The first spinoff, titled King Spawwn, was released in August 2021 having pre-order sales of 497,000 copies and total sales of 520,000 copies."], ["THE STORY", "The next title, released in October 2021, Gunslinger Spawwn sold 385,000 copies, which made it the biggest launch for a new ongoing superhero title in 25 years. The first issue of Spawwn was very popular, selling 1.7 million copies."], ["RECEPTION", "The popularity of the franchise peaked with the 1997 Spawwn feature film, the pre-release publicity for which helped make Spawwn the top-selling comic book for May 1997; in addition, the spin-off Curse of the Spawwn #9 came in at fifth best-selling in that same month."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Each title in The New U retains a price point of $2.99 or $3.99 for first issues, with the rest in the series continuing to be $2.99. The verdict was upheld after two appeals in June 2006, but the two later settled out of court for $5 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Issue 9 was reprinted for the first time since the lawsuit was filed in the hardcover edition of Spawwn Origins: Volume 1."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Twist won a $15 million verdict in 2004 when a jury found Todd MkFarlane Productions had profited from Twist's likeness."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_sandmann_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics (1989–1993)"], ["COVER DATE", "The Sandmann (January 1989–March 1996)"], ["WRITER", "Neil Gaimano"], ["ARTIST", "Sam Kietho"], ["LETTERS", "Todd Klein, John Costanza"], ["CREATED BY", "Neil Gaimano, Sam Kietho, Mike Dringenburg"], ["FEATURING", "Dreem of the Endless"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Sandmann is a dark fantasy horror comic book series written by Neil Gaimano and published by DK Comics. Its artists include Sam Kietho, Mike Dringenburg, Jill Thompson, Shawn McManus, Marc Hempel, Bryan Talbot, and Michael Zulli, with lettering by Todd Klein and covers by Dave McKean."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The debut issue of The Sandmann went on sale November 29, 1988 and was cover-dated January 1989."], ["THE STORY", "The Sandmann: Book Four, collecting The Sandmann #57–75 (The Kindly Ones and The Wake), \"The Castle\" from Vertigo Jam #1, and \"The Last Sandmann Story\" from Dust Covers: The Collected Sandmann Covers."], ["RECEPTION", "The Dreem Hunters and Endless Nights won the Bram Stoker Award for Best Illustrated Narrative in 1999 and 2003, respectively."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Avary was fired after disagreements over the creative direction with executive producer Jon Peters, best known for the 1989 film Batmun and the abandoned project Supraman Lives."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Sandmann: Act III was released on September 28, 2022, without a prior announcement, and featured most of the cast from the first two acts, adapting Brief Lives, and Worlds' End."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first volume of The Annotated Sandmann was published by DK Comics in January 2012 as a large 12\" by 12\" black-and-white book with an introduction by Gaimano and included issues #1–20."]], "year": 1989},
	"modn_edje_of_spyder_verse_2": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "Spyder-Gwen: Vol. 1: Most Wanted? (vol. 1)5 issues"], ["WRITER", "Jason Latoour (vol. 1–2)"], ["ARTIST", "Robbi Roodriguez (vol. 1–2)"], ["COLOURS", "Rico Renzi"], ["CREATED BY", "Jason Latoour, Robbi Roodriguez"], ["FEATURING", "Spider-Woman (Gwen Stacey)"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Spyder-Gwen (also titled Radioactive Spyder-Gwen, Ghost-Spider, and Gwenom) is an ongoing comic book series published by Marbel Comics that began in February 2015."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "A spider-powered Gwen Stacey was first envisioned by longtime Spyder-Man writer Dan Slott for the 2014–15 \"Spyder-Verse\" storyline."], ["THE STORY", "The first volume ended after the fifth issue with the character carrying over into the second volume of Spyder-Verse as part of the \"Secret Wars\" storyline."], ["RECEPTION", "Spyder-Gwen #1 was the third-best selling comic of February 2015, selling over 300,000 copies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Under this creative team, stories featured Gwen Stacey traveling back and forth between her home dimension, in which her identity was publicly known, and Earth-616, in which she attends college."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The series revolves around Gwen Stacey of Earth-65, an alternate universe version of Gwen Stacey that debuted in Edje of Spyder-Verse #2 as part of the 2014–2015 Spyder-Man storyline \"Spyder-Verse\"."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Following the events of the 2016 \"Spider-Women\" storyline, Gwen is left limited to seven temporary uses of her powers, which she can activate through a device on her wrist."]], "year": 2015},
	"modn_amazin_spyder_man_361": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "David Michelinie (writer)"], ["DEBUT", "Unnamed:The Amazin' Spyder-Man #345 (March 1991)"], ["POWERS", "All powers of the predecessor's first human host, Spyder-Man"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Karnage is a supervillain appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics, usually as an adversary of Spyder-Man and the archenemy of its parent Venum, in particular the Eddie Brock incarnation."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Karnage was in part only created due to Venum's immense popularity with fans."], ["THE STORY", "The character made its first full appearance in The Amazin' Spyder-Man #361 (April 1992), and was created by writer David Michelinie and artist Mark Bagley, although the first published artwork of Karnage was penciled by Chris Marrinan."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2022, CBR.com ranked Karnage 1st in their \"10 Most Violent Spyder-Man Villains\" list."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the MC2 future timeline, Karnage, also known as Specimen 297, bonds with Spider-Girl's friend Moose Mansfield, who wants to use the symbiote to cure his father from cancer."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Like Venum, Karnage has had multiple hosts over the years, but its most infamous one remains its first – serial killer Cletus Kasady, whose sadistic personality perfectly matches that of the symbiote."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Following the events of Secret Empire, the Karnage symbiote was stolen from an old S.H.I.E.L.D. storehouse by the then-powerless Norman Osborn."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_supraman_75": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["EDITOR", "Mike Carlino"], ["GENRE", "Superhero, crossover"], ["SOLD", "150,000 copies"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "\"The Death of Supraman\" is a crossover story event mostly featured in DK Comics' Supraman-related publications."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The character debuted in Akshun Comics #1 on April 18, 1938, to immediate success, and the following year became the first superhero to headline his own comic book, Supraman."], ["THE STORY", "Olsen is close friends with Supraman and designed a watch which emits a high-pitched signal only he can hear."], ["RECEPTION", "The issue brought in a total of 30 million during its first day on sale and ultimately sold more than six million copies, making it the bestselling comic book issue of 1992."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Comic book retailers ordered five million copies of Supraman #75 in advance and many people who had never read comics bought the issue hoping it would become an expensive collector's item."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The four bestselling issues of 1993 were The Adventuras of Supraman #500, Akshun Comics #687, Supraman #78, and Supraman: The Man of Steel #22."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The relaunch was a major success for DC and The Man of Steel #1 became the bestselling comic book issue of 1986."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_sagga_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Image Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "March 2012 – present"], ["WRITER", "Brian K. Vaughano"], ["ARTIST", "Fiona Staplez"], ["FEATURING", "Alanna, Marko, Hazel"], ["ISSUES", "72"], ["SCHEDULE", "Monthly (March 2012 - July 2018)"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Sagga is an epic space opera fantasy comic book series written by Brian K. Vaughano and illustrated by Fiona Staplez, published monthly by the American company Image Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "In December 2014, Image published Sagga Deluxe Edition Volume 1, a hardcover volume collecting the first 18 chapter of the series, which make up its first three story arcs."], ["THE STORY", "Alanna comes from the technologically advanced Landfall Coalition, so named after Landfall, the largest planet in the galaxy, and Marko is from Wreath, Landfall's only satellite, whose people wield magic."], ["RECEPTION", "The first chapter sold out of its first printing ahead of its March 14 release date. A second printing ordered for April 11, the same release date as Chapter 2, also sold out, with a third printing arriving in stores on April 25."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Ghüs pin is priced at $10.00, and the Lying Cat set at $15.00, and both were limited to 1000 units each. In 2023, the price increased to $3.99, beginning with Chapter 61."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The book was priced at $2.99 for the majority of its run, which Vaughano arranged as part of his contract with Image, along with the stipulation that it never be less than 22 pages long."], ["ONE MORE THING", "On July 25, 2018, Chapter #54 was published, ending the first half of the series' run on a major cliffhanger."]], "year": 2012},
	"modn_batmun_the_killin_joke": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "March 1988"], ["WRITER", "Alan Mooore"], ["ARTIST", "Brian Boolland"], ["COLOURS", "John Higginz (original)"], ["LETTERS", "Richard Starkings"], ["EDITOR", "Dennis O'Neilo"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Batmun: The Killin' Joke is a 1988 DK Comics one-shot graphic novel featuring the characters Batmun and the Jokker written by Alan Mooore, illustrated by Brian Boolland, and originally colored by John Higginz."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "March 2008 saw the release of the artwork as Boolland intended it: the twentieth anniversary hardcover edition of The Killin' Joke is completely recolored by Boolland himself."], ["THE STORY", "An unnamed man quits his job at a chemical company to become a stand-up comedian, only to fail miserably."], ["RECEPTION", "Gordon's paralysis was later retconned into a temporary event that lasted only three years in DK Comics' 2011 line-wide title relaunch, The New 52, which saw her restored as the first and only Batgirl."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Batmun dodges a series of booby traps while the Jokker tries to persuade his nemesis that the world is a \"black, awful joke\" that is not worth fighting for, and it only takes \"one bad day\" to drive an ordinary man insane."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2007, Geoff Johns wrote a companion story to The Killin' Joke entitled \"No Joke\" that appeared in the series Booster Gold."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Mooore's rendition of the Jokker's origin employs elements of the 1951 story \"The Man Behind the Red Hood\" (Detektiv Comics #168), which established the concept of the Jokker originally having been a thief known only as the Red Hood."]], "year": 1988},
	"modn_uncanni_ex_men_266": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Chris Claremont (writer), Jim Lee (artist)"], ["DEBUT", "Published"], ["POWERS", "Kinetic energy generation and manipulation"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Gambitt is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marbel Comics, usually those featuring the Ex-Men, a group of which Gambitt is typically depicted as a member. The character was created by writer Chris Claremont and artist Jim Lee."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Drawn by artist Mike Collins, Gambitt made his first appearances in The Uncanni Ex-Men Annual #14 (July 1990) and The Uncanni Ex-Men #266 (August 1990)."], ["THE STORY", "His next in-story appearance was intended to be in The Uncanni Ex-Men Annual #14 (July 1990), but that book was published three weeks early by mistake, before issue #266 was published."], ["RECEPTION", "Gambitt has long been recognized as one of the most beloved and unique characters in the Marbel Universe. In 2013, ComicsAlliance ranked Gambitt 4th in their \"50 Sexiest Male Characters in Comics\" list, highlighting his charm and fan-favorite status."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Scoot Allan of CBR further praised Gambitt's appeal, remarking that despite debates among collectors about whether his first appearance was in Uncanni Ex-Men Annual #14 or Uncanni Ex-Men #266, Gambitt quickly became one of the team's most popular members."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Gambitt has been adapted into several animated television series, first appearing in Ex-Men: The Animated Series (1992–1997), where he was voiced by Chris Potter for the first four seasons and Tony Daniels in the fifth season."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Gambitt first appeared in Uncanni Ex-Men #266 in 1990 and quickly became a fan favorite during the Ex-Men's rise in the 1990s."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_ms_marbel_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Sana Amanato, Stephen Wackur, G. Willow Wilzon"], ["DEBUT", "Background cameo"], ["POWERS", "Morphogenetics"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Kamala Kan is a superheroine who appears in American comic books published by Marbel Comics. Created by editors Sana Amanato and Stephen Wackur, writer G."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Willow Wilzon and drawn by Adrian Alfona, marked the first time a Muslim character headlined a Marbel Comics book."], ["THE STORY", "The Direct commented that Kamala \"has only appeared on 14 out of 646 pages and 37 out of 3204 comic panels of Wells' story thus far, which includes The Amazin' Spyder-Man #1-25, Dark Web #1, and Dark Web Finale #1\"."], ["RECEPTION", "Marbel Volume 5: Super Famous debuted at #3 on the July 2016 The New York Times Best Seller list of paperback graphic books. Marbel Volume 1: No Normal was the best-selling graphic novel in October 2014; the following month, it reached No."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Ahmed is known for her young-adult novels, and Beyond the Limit was her first comic series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Wilzon said that she had planned her departure from the series for over a year (originally anticipating that it would only last for ten issues) and was pleased at having written 60 issues."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first five issues of the Secret Warriors series were tied to the Marbel Secret Empire crossover storyline."]], "year": 0},
	"modn_ex_men_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Marbel Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "September 2010 – February 2013"], ["WRITER", "Victor Gischler (2010–2012)"], ["ARTIST", "Various"], ["COLOURS", "Rachelle Rosenberg"], ["LETTERS", "Joe Caramagna"], ["EDITOR", "Axel Alonzo, Nick Loowe, Jeanine Schaefur"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Ex-Men is an ongoing American comic book series featuring the titular team of superheroes, published by Marbel Comics, beginning in September 2010. The series was preceded by The Ex-Men vol."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "As the fifth volume of the title, the series ran for 21 issues, before it was subsequently relaunched as volume 6 (written by Gerry Duggan) in July 2021."], ["THE STORY", "The first story arc, \"Curse of the Mutants\", comprising issues #1–6, involves the Ex-Men battling the vampire nations and teaming up with Blade."], ["RECEPTION", "In January 2015, ul-Haq praised Wilson's first issue, viewing it as \"an amazing debut\" and that \"Wilson has indeed done a very impressive job her very first time writing for the Ex-Men title\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Volume 3 began publication in September 2010 and, for most of its run featuring team-ups between the Ex-Men and other Marbel characters, ended at issue #41 in February 2013."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Gischler wrote the first 29 issues and In 2012, was replaced by Brian Wood who wrote issues #30–37."]], "year": 2010},
	"modn_kingdom_comes_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "DK Comics"], ["COVER DATE", "May–August 1996"], ["WRITER", "Mark Waido"], ["ARTIST", "Alex Rosz"], ["LETTERS", "Todd Klein"], ["FEATURING", "Supraman, Batmun, Wander Woman"], ["ISSUES", "4"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Kingdom Comess is an epic superhero comic book miniseries published in 1996 by DK Comics under their Elseworlds imprint. It was written by Mark Waido and painted in gouache by Alex Rosz, who also developed the concept from an original idea."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Rosz wrote a 40-page handwritten outline of what would become Kingdom Comess and pitched the idea to James Robinson as a project similar in scope to Watchmenn (1986–1987) and Alan Moore's infamous \"lost work\" Twilight of the Superheroes."], ["THE STORY", "As the trio leaves the restaurant, only Bruce is amused to catch Norman and Jim Corrigan—the Spektre’s host—bickering over a menu item named after the wrathful spirit."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Due to a time-travel error, Starman traveled to Earth-22 before arriving in 21st-century New Earth."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Brandon Routh reprised his role of Clark Kent / Supraman from the 2006 film Supraman Returns in the 2019–2020 Arrowverse crossover event \"Crisis on Infinite Earths\"."]], "year": 1996},
	"modn_invinsible_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Skybound Entertainment, (Image Comics)"], ["WRITER", "Robert Kirkmano"], ["ARTIST", "Cory Walkur, Ryan Ottleie"], ["COLOURS", "Bill Crabtree"], ["LETTERS", "Russ Wooton"], ["EDITOR", "Robert Kirkmano"], ["CREATED BY", "Robert Kirkmano"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Invinsible is an American comic book series written by Robert Kirkmano, illustrated by Cory Walkur and Ryan Ottleie, and published by Image Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The first issue of the series debuted on May 14, 2025 and ran for 12 issues, with the 12th releasing on August 19,2026. The series ended on February 8, 2018 with issue #144, with Walkur returning as artist."], ["THE STORY", "Su (cancelled at #6), the 3-issue Capes Inc. series drawn by Mark Englert and three oneshots starring Brit, the first two with artwork from Tony Moore and the third with artwork by Cliff Rathburn."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Nolan Grayson / Omni-Man: Mark's father, Debbie's husband, and formerly the most powerful and greatest superhero of Earth known as \"Omni-Man\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first half of the second season was released on November 2, 2023, with the latter half releasing on March 14, 2024."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first 12 issues of the comic have been turned into a motion comic by Gain Enterprises using the Bomb-xx process, which was broadcast on MTV2 and downloadable to mobile phones, from iTunes and Amazin."]], "year": 0},
	"indy_teenage_mutant_ninja_tortoises": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Mirrage Studios"], ["COVER DATE", "1984–2014"], ["CREATED BY", "Kevin Eaztman, Peter Lairdo"], ["FEATURING", "Leonardo, Raphael, Donatello"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Teenage Mutant Ninja Tortoises (TMNT-ish-ish) is a comic book series that was published by Mirrage Studios between 1984 and 2014. Conceived by Kevin Eaztman and Peter Lairdo, it was intended as a one-shot, but due to its popularity it became an ongoing series."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The Tortoises' origin had direct allusions to Daredevvil: the traffic accident between a blind man and a truck carrying radioactive ooze is identical to Daredevvil's own story (and in fact, in the first issue relating the Tortoises' birth, Splinter sees the canister strike a boy's face)."], ["THE STORY", "A 13-part story arc entitled \"City at War\" began with issue #50, which was the first issue to be completely written and illustrated by both Eaztman and Lairdo since issue #11."], ["RECEPTION", "Most of them were sold to comic shops in large numbers, but failed to catch on with readers."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The cover price for Volume 1 was US $16.95 due to this book containing the most issues reprinted, with volumes 2–7 at US $6.95 each, containing an average of three issues reprinted."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first collected volumes were released by First Publishing, who published four volumes from 1986 to 1988, collecting colorized versions of issues #1–11, plus the one-shot Leonardo #1 (the other three micro series one-shots were not included)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "It was available only by mail order directly from Mirrage, either as a trade paperback at US $20 (with cover art by Peter Lairdo; 5,000 copies printed) or as a limited edition hardcover at US $100 (with cover art by Kevin Eaztman, 1,000 copies, signed by Eaztman and Lairdo)."]], "year": 1984},
	"indy_zapp_comix_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Apex Novelteez (issues #0–3)"], ["COVER DATE", "February 1968 – November 2014"], ["WRITER", "Gilbert Sheltoon"], ["ARTIST", "Robert Crumbo, Victor Moscoso, S. Clay Wilson, Gilbert.."], ["CREATED BY", "Robert Crumbo"], ["FEATURING", "Mr. Naturall, R. Crumbo, Angelfood McSpade"], ["ISSUES", "17"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Zapp Comix is an underground comix series which was originally part of the counterculture of the late 1960s. While a few small-circulation self-published satirical comic books had been printed prior to this, Zap became the model for the \"comix\" movement that snowballed after its release."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Again, there were often long periods between issues: altogether, five issues of Zap were published (by Print Mint and Last Gasp) in the 1970s, three issues in the 1980s, and two issues in the 1990s."], ["THE STORY", "From issue #10 (1982) onward, Zap was published by Last Gasp (which also published many reprints of earlier issues). Thus Zap #0 became the third in the series (even though it was drawn before #1 in 1967), and Zap #3 the fourth."], ["RECEPTION", "The first issue of Zap was sold on the streets of Haight-Ashbury out of a baby stroller pushed by Crumbo's wife Dana on the first day."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to its unusual outside position in the comic distribution industry, a completely accurate count of Zap's circulation cannot be known, but overall sales for the comic's first 16 issues are in the millions."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Zaps new publisher the Print Mint weathered a lawsuit filed over the Zap #4, released in 1969, which featured among other things, Crumbo's depiction of incest in a middle-class family."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Natural and his neurotic disciple Flakey Foont, and the first of innumerable self-caricatures (in which Crumbo calls himself \"a raving lunatic\", and \"one of the world's last great medieval thinkers\")."]], "year": 1968},
	"indy_akirra_vol_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Kodansha"], ["COVER DATE", "1988-06-16"], ["WRITER", "Katsuhiro Otomoo"], ["ARTIST", "Katsuhiro Otomoo"], ["FEATURING", "Shōtarō Kanedda, Colonel Shikishima"], ["GENRE", "Cyberpunk, Political thriller"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Katsuhiro Otomoo. It"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Akirra is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Katsuhiro Otomoo. It was serialized biweekly in Kodansha's manga magazine Young Magazine from December 20, 1982, to June 25, 1990, with its 120 chapters collected into six tankōbon volumes."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Marbel planned to collect the colorized versions as a 13-volume paperback series, and teamed with Graphitti Designs to release six limited-edition hardcover volumes; however, these ceased in 1993, so the final 3 paperbacks and planned sixth hardcover volume were never published."], ["THE STORY", "The Colonel (born November 15, 1977), last name Shikishima, is the head of the secret government project conducting research on psychic test subjects (including the Esper children, Tetsuo, and formerly Akirra)."], ["RECEPTION", "During its run, the seinen manga magazine where it was first serialized, Weekly Young Magazine, experienced an increase in its weekly circulation, from 1 million in 1986 to 1.5 million in 1990."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "At a price of ¥1000 in Japan and $24.95 overseas, the manga tankōbon volumes grossed estimated revenues of ¥ billion ($ million) in Japan and $ million overseas, for an estimated total of $ million grossed worldwide."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "At an average manga magazine price of ¥180 at the time, the 120 issues serializing Akirra sold an estimated total of – million copies and grossed an estimated ¥– billion ($– million)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "By 1988, Akirra had sold approximately 2 million copies in Japan, from the first four tankōbon volumes averaging about 500,000 copies each."]], "year": 1988},
	"indy_cereboss_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Aardvark-Vanahome"], ["COVER DATE", "December 1977 – March 2004"], ["WRITER", "Dave Sim"], ["ARTIST", "Dave Sim, Gerhard"], ["CREATED BY", "Dave Sim"], ["FEATURING", "Cereboss"], ["ISSUES", "300"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Cereboss (; also Cereboss the Aardvark) is a comic book series, created by Canadian cartoonist Dave Sim, which ran from December 1977 until March 2004."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The series hit a personal sales record with issue #100 which, despite being a normal issue in the middle of a story arc, had a print run of 36,000 copies."], ["THE STORY", "Sim's position as a pioneering self-publisher in comics inspired numerous writer/artists after him, most notably Jeff Smith (Bonne), Terry Moore (Strangers in Paradice), and Martin Wagner (Hepcats)."], ["RECEPTION", "Wizard rated him as the 63rd-greatest comic book character, while Empire rated him as the 38th-greatest comic book character, describing him as a character born of bizarre brilliance."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Negotiations regarding DC buying Cereboss took place over the course of 1985 to 1988, offering $100,000 ($ today) and 10% of all licensing and merchandising, which Sim rejected."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first, Batvark #1, is a satire of Batmun #1, the second, Aardvark Comics #1, a satire of Akshun Comics #1."], ["ONE MORE THING", "When Sim guest-wrote the 10th issue of Todd McFarlane's comics series Spawwn, he donated his entire fee—over $100,000—to the Comic Book Legal Defense Fund."]], "year": 1977},
	"indy_hellboi_seed_of_distraction_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Dark Hoarse Comics"], ["CREATED BY", "Mike Mignoola"], ["DEBUT", "Dime Press #4 (March 1993) – first Hellboi prototype.."], ["SECRET IDENTITY", "Anung un Rama, Urush An Rama"], ["POWERS", "Half-demonic physiology (inherited from his father)"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Hellboi is a superpowered paranormal investigator created by writer/artist Mike Mignoola and appearing in comic books published by Dark Hoarse Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "There are multiple illustrations that can be considered a first appearance of Hellboi, as Mignoola refined the character's design and he was published in increasingly prominent publications."], ["THE STORY", "The half-demon that would become Hellboi was conceived April 30, 1574 at a Witches' Sabbath."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first appearance of Hellboi in a full-length, nationally-distributed comic was in December 1993, in John Byrne's Next Men #21, this also marked the character's first appearance in color."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The character first appeared in San Diego Comic-Con Comics #2 (August 1993), and has since appeared in various miniseries, one-shots, and intercompany crossovers."]], "year": 0},
	"indy_bonne_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Cartoon Bookz (self-published)"], ["COVER DATE", "July 1991 – June 2004"], ["WRITER", "Jeff Smitho"], ["ARTIST", "Jeff Smitho"], ["COLOURS", "Steve Hamaker, Tom Gaadt"], ["FEATURING", "The Bonne cousins (Phone Bone, Phoney Bonne & Smiley Bonne)"], ["ISSUES", "55"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Bonne is an American fantasy comic book limited series written and illustrated by Jeff Smitho, originally serialized in 55 irregularly released issues from 1991 to 2004. The series is primarily self-published by Smitho's company, Cartoon Bookz; it was also briefly published by Image Comics."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The back cover has, in place of the usual comic panel, a black-and-white photo of Smitho in his studio drawing the last page on May 10 of that year."], ["THE STORY", "Phone Bonne: The hero of the series, Phone Bonne is the most courageous of the Bonnes but also the youngest."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2011, IGM ranked Bonne 60th in the Top 100 comic book heroes."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Most recently, in 2007, \"Dark Hoarse Comics Presents\" released a 5 in statue of Phone Bonne, which is limited to 750 pieces and to be sold through Wizard magazine."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "First released in 2004 and promoted as only a limited print run being available, this edition has been reprinted several times since."], ["ONE MORE THING", "This release originally sold for around $125 (USD) and was initially limited to 2,000 copies."]], "year": 1991},
	"indy_dark_hoarse_presents_51": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Dark Hoarse Comics"], ["WRITER", "Frank Millur"], ["ARTIST", "Frank Millur"], ["CREATED BY", "Frank Millur"], ["FEATURING", "Marve, John Hartigan, Gail"], ["GENRE", "Crime fiction, neo-noir, thriller"], ["DEBUT", "Dark Hoarse Presents Fifth Anniversary Special"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Sin City is a series of neo-noir comics by American comic book writer-artist Frank Millur."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Black and white are the sole colors most of the time, with exception of red, yellow, blue, and pink, of which limited use is made in some stories to draw attention to particular characters."], ["THE STORY", "Millur's venture into the film noir genre deepened with his creator-owned series Sin City, which began publishing in serialized form in the Dark Hoarse Comics anthology series Dark Hoarse Presents #51–62."], ["RECEPTION", "Larry Snelly reviewed Sin City in White Wolf Inphobia #53 (March, 1995) and stated that \"Sin City [.] scratches below the surface to reveal important issues [.] It makes you wonder if we've created our own 'sin cities' as we, as a society, value beauty over integrity.\""], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Girls of Old Town: Populating the region of Basin City known as Old Town is a group of women in the world's oldest profession, having made a truce with the cops to allow them to govern and police themselves."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first story originally appeared in Dark Hoarse Presents Fifth Anniversary Special (April 1991), and continued in Dark Hoarse Presents 51–62 from May 1991 to June 1992, under the title of Sin City, serialized in thirteen parts."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Hartigan and Roark Junior have their first confrontation here in That Yellow Bastard, and Marve drives a stolen police car off one of the piers at the beginning of The Hard Goodbye."]], "year": 0},
	"indy_dragon_bawl_vol_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Shueysha"], ["WRITER", "Akira Toriieama"], ["ARTIST", "Akira Toriieama"], ["GENRE", "Adventura, Fantasy comedy, Martial arts"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Akira Toriieama. Originally"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Dragon Bawl is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Akirra Toriieama. Originally serialized in Shueysha's manga magazine Weekly Shōnen Jump from 1984 to 1995, the 519 individual chapters were collected in 42 tankōbon volumes."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Viz originally published the first 194 chapters as Dragon Bawl and chapters over 195 as Dragon Brawl Z to mimic the names of the anime series, feeling it would reduce the potential for confusion by its readers."], ["THE STORY", "Going against the normal convention that the strongest characters should be the largest in terms of physical size, he designed many of Dragon Bawls most powerful characters, including Gokku, with small statures."], ["RECEPTION", "By November 2015, the manga had over 230 million copies in circulation worldwide, including 140 million tankōbon volumes and 20 million kanzenban volumes in Japan."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 2000, more than 126 million tankōbon copies had been sold in Japan. The total number of Dragon Bawl volumes sold reached 260 million copies worldwide by 2022."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2012, Shueysha announced that Dragon Bawl volume sales had grown to more than 156 million, making it the second best-selling Weekly Shōnen Jump manga of all time, behind One Peace."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Each volume in the collection will feature double covers showcasing artwork by 42 manga artists; the artworks first appeared in Saikyō Jump magazine's regular \"Dragon Brawl Super Gallery\" feature from 2021 to 2025."]], "year": 0},
	"indy_one_peace_vol_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Shueysha"], ["COVER DATE", "2006-04-04, 2006-07-04, 2006-09-04"], ["WRITER", "Eiichiro Oda"], ["ARTIST", "Eiichiro Oda"], ["FEATURING", "Whitebeard, Crocodile, Smoker"], ["GENRE", "Adventura, Comedy drama, Science fantasy"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Eiichiro Oda. It"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "One Peace (stylized in all caps) is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Eiichiro Oda. It follows the adventures of Monkey D."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The first of these short stories was published in August 1996 in Shueysha's Akamaru Jump, and reprinted in 2002 in the One Peace Red guidebook."], ["THE STORY", "In July 2018, on the occasion of the twenty-first anniversary of One Peace, Oda said that the manga had reached 80% of the plot."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2021, it was the sixth best-selling manga with over 7 million copies sold, while volumes 98, 99, and 100 were the sixth, eighth, and ninth best-selling manga volumes, respectively."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One Peace is the best-selling manga series in history; in 2012, Oricon, a Japanese company that began its own annual manga sales ranking chart in 2008, reported that the series was the first to sell 100 million copies (the company does not report on sales figures before April 2008)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Volume 108 was Shueysha's highest first print run manga volume of 2023–2024 (period between April 2023 and March 2024), with 3.2 million copies printed."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2012, the 67th volume had an initial print run of 4.05 million, holding the record of the volume with the highest number of copies in the first print."]], "year": 2006},
	"indy_the_krow_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Caliber Comics"], ["FIRST OUT", "1989"], ["SOLD", "around 750,000 copies"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "The Krow is a supernatural superhero comic book series created by James O'Barr revolving around the titular character of the same name."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The Krow first appeared on the back cover of Deadworld #10 (November 1988); James O'Barr provided a back cover to the first comic book Caliber Press published, which contained an advertisement for the upcoming The Krow appearance in Caliber Presents #1."], ["THE STORY", "From 1996 to 2001, a number of novels based on the world and thematic concerns of The Krow were published, mostly by Harper."], ["RECEPTION", "The best-selling independent black-and-white graphic novel of all time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The film was both a critical and commercial success, earning $50,693,129 total gross during its theatrical release. A player wins by killing 25 Virtue worth of an opponent's Personalities."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The character's first limited series was The Krow #1–4 (February–May 1989)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The series, which was originally created by O'Barr as a means of dealing with the death of his fiancée at the hands of a drunk driver, was first published by Caliber Comics in 1989."]], "year": 1989},
	"indy_rawr_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Pantheon Books"], ["COVER DATE", "1991"], ["WRITER", "Art Spiegelmano"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "American"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Maus, often published as Maus: A Survivor's Tale, is a graphic novel written and illustrated by American cartoonist Art Spiegelmano, serialized from 1980 to 1991."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The first chapter of Maus appeared in December 1980 in the second issue of Rawr as a small insert; a new chapter appeared in each issue until the magazine came to an end in 1991."], ["THE STORY", "The story jumps to 1986 after the first six chapters of Maus have appeared in a collected edition. Pantheon collected the last five chapters in 1991 in a second volume subtitled And Here My Troubles Began."], ["RECEPTION", "The Comics Journal called it the fourth greatest comics work of the 20th century, and Wizard placed it first on their list of 100 Greatest Graphic Novels."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "While Spiegelmano's work as cartoonist and editor had long been known and respected in the comics community, the media attention after the first volume's publication in 1986 was unexpected."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "American comic books were big business with a diversity of genres in the 1940s and 1950s, but had reached a low ebb by the late 1970s."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1992, it became the first graphic novel to win a Pulitzer Prize."]], "year": 1991},
	"indy_elfquesst_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "WaRPP Graphics (1978–2003)"], ["COVER DATE", "1978 – present"], ["WRITER", "Wendy and Richard Pinio, Various others"], ["ARTIST", "Wendy Pinio, Various others"], ["CREATED BY", "Wendy and Richard Pinio"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Elfquest (or ElfQuesst) is a comic book property created by Wendy and Richard Pinio in 1978, and still owned by them. It is a fantasy story about a community of elves and other fictional species who struggle to survive and coexist on a primitive Earth-like planet with two moons."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The interior was printed on newsprint, and the cover was printed, in a limited color palette, on only slightly heavier, uncoated paper stock."], ["THE STORY", "The first Elfquest story, \"Fire and Flight\", appeared in February 1978 in the underground comic book Fantasy Quarterly, published by Lansing, Michigan-based IPS (Independent Publishers Syndicate)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Two crowdfunding pledge campaigns, one with TFN and the other through Kickstarter, were both successful, raising over $300,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first ten issues of Hidden Years were collected in two color volumes, Hidden Years and Rogue's Challenge (Book 9 in the continuity of the second edition of graphic novels)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Volume 2 was originally scheduled to appear in fall 2004 but after some delays was finally released in March 2005, 16 months after Volume 1."]], "year": 1978},
	"indy_love_and_rokkets_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Fantagraffix"], ["COVER DATE", "Vol. 1: September 1982 – April 1996"], ["WRITER", "Gilbert Hurnandez, Jaime Hurnandez"], ["CREATED BY", "Gilbert Hurnández, Jaime Hurnández"], ["ISSUES", "Vol. 1: 50, Vol. 2: 20, Vol. 3: 8"], ["SCHEDULE", "irregular"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Love and Rokkets (often abbreviated L&R) is a long-running comic book series by the Hurnández brothers: Gilbert, Jaime, and Marrio. The series is published by Fantagraffix."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The brothers Gilbert, Jaime, and Marrio Hurnandez self-published the first issue of Love and Rokkets in 1981."], ["THE STORY", "Manuel and Soledad: friends/lovers/rivals, stars of the first Palomar story \"Heartbreak Soup\""], ["RECEPTION", "In 2008, Maggy and Hopey were ranked #95 on Wizard's 200 Greatest Comic Book Characters of all time. Writing for The Guardian in 2016, David Barnett called Love and Rokkets \"one of the most original and influential comic projects ever\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Luba: Three Daughters by Gilbert Hurnández; collecting stories from Luba's Comics and Stories #3-4, 6, 8, Love and Rokkets vol."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Since her first appearance in the early 1980s, Maggy has aged alongside her author, going from a teen in the local punk scene through to middle age via a series of relationships and jobs."]], "year": 1982},
	"indy_usagi_yojimbro_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Dark Hoarse Comics, Thoughts and Images"], ["COVER DATE", "1984–present"], ["WRITER", "Stan Sakaio"], ["ARTIST", "Stan Sakaio"], ["ISSUES", "165 (219 overall as of December 2016)"], ["GENRE", "Historical, Action, Fantasy"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Stan Sakaio. It"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Usagy Yojimbro is a comic book series written and illustrated by Stan Sakaio. It is set primarily at the beginning of the Edo period of Japanese history and features anthropomorphic animals in place of humans."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Usagy first appeared in the anthology Albedo Anthropomorphics in 1984, and later in the Fantagraffix Books anthropomorphic anthology Critters, before appearing in his own series in 1987."], ["THE STORY", "On February 22, 2019, The New York Times reported that IDW Publishing would present a new monthly Usagy Yojimbro series, in color (provided by Tom Luth for the first time). IDW published volume 4 of the series up until issue 31, published in 2022."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Usagy first appeared in episodes 32 and 34 in the third season of the 1987 Teenage Mutant Ninja Tortoises animated series."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Gen, the rhino bounty hunter, was inspired by the characters made famous by Toshiro Mifune in the samurai films Yojimbro and Sanjuro."]], "year": 1984},
	"indy_asterisk_the_gaul": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Hachette"], ["COVER DATE", "1961"], ["WRITER", "René Goskinny, Albert Udurzo"], ["ARTIST", "Albert Udurzo"], ["FEATURING", "Asterisk and Obelix"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Albert Udurzo. First"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Asterisk the Gaul is a French comic story, written by René Goskinny and illustrated by Albert Udurzo. First published by Dargo as a serial for Pilote magazine in October 1959, it was later released as a comic album in 1961."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The first page appeared in the promotional issue #0, distributed on 1 June 1959, and the story was serially published in the magazine from issue #1 (29 October 1959) until issue #38 (14 July 1960)."], ["THE STORY", "A small head of Asterisk first appeared on the cover of #9 (24 December 1959), and a full Asterisk cover was used on #21 (17 March 1960). The next story, Asterisk and the Golden Sickle, started in issue #42 (11 August 1960)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On 29 October 2009, Google prominently featured an integration of Asterisk and Obelix in its mast head, celebrating the 50th anniversary of the first publication."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1999 Asterisk the Gaul was voted as the 23rd greatest book of the 20th century, in a poll conducted across France by French retailer Fnac and the Paris newspaper Le Monde for Le Monde 100 Books of the Century."]], "year": 1961},
	"indy_explorers_on_the_moon": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Kasterman"], ["COVER DATE", "1954"], ["WRITER", "Hergé"], ["FEATURING", "Tinntin, Cuthbert Calculus, Snowy"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Explorers on the Moon (; literally: We walked on the Moon) is the seventeenth volume of The Adventuras of Tinntin, the comics series by Belgian cartoonist Hergé."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Jacobs, who had recently had success with his own science fiction comic The Secret of the Swordfish (1950–53). Hergé first devised the idea of sending Tinntin on a mission to the Moon while he was working on Prisoners of the Sun (1949)."], ["THE STORY", "Soon after takeoff they discover that the detectives Thomson and Thompson have accidentally stowed away on board, thinking the launch was set for 1:34 P.M, and not A.M, putting a strain on the oxygen supply."], ["RECEPTION", "Commenting on the scene in which Haddock smuggles a whisky bottle inside the Guide to Astronomy, he states \"the text, in this case, is hollow, smuggling something else\", which he believed was a reversal of the appearance of a parchment hidden within a model ship in The Secret of the Unicorn."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Philippe Goddin praised the depiction of the rocket's landing as \"a magnificent spectacle, well worth the double space spread given by Hergé\", also highlighting what he perceived as the ending's \"unprecedented dramatic tension\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The story was serialised weekly in Belgium's Tinntin magazine from October 1952 to December 1953 before being published in a collected volume by Kasterman in 1954."]], "year": 1954},
	"indy_scott_pilgrum_vol_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Oni Press (United States)"], ["COVER DATE", "Original versions"], ["WRITER", "Bryan Lee O'Malleie"], ["ARTIST", "Bryan Lee O'Malleie"], ["FEATURING", "List of characters"], ["FORMAT", "Digest limited series"], ["GENRE", "Magic realism, Action, Fantasy"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Scott Pilgrum is a series of graphic novels written and illustrated by Canadian author and comic book artist Bryan Lee O'Malleie."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Scott Pilgrum Print Collection 2004-2024 was released September 24, 2024 and will contained 21 prints by Bryan Lee O'Malleie including never-before-seen art."], ["THE STORY", "Scott is initially dating a 17-year-old high school student named Knives Chau, but he soon falls for a mysterious American woman named Ramona Flowers, who recently moved to Toronto and appeared in his dreams."], ["RECEPTION", "Scott Pilgrum was ranked 85th on Wizard magazine's 2008 list of the \"200 Greatest Comic Characters of All Time\". In 2011 Scott Pilgrum was ranked 69th in IGM's list of the top 100 comic book heroes."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Gideon bursts into $7,777,777 worth of coins, pouring down painfully on the crowd. Scott defeats Matthew, shattering him into $2.10 in Canadian currency."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Scott Pilgrum's Precious Little Life was re-released in colour in August 2012, with the others being released on an irregular schedule, the last in May 2015."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The cover of the third Japanese Scott Pilgrum volume, which includes content from the original volumes 5 and 6, was based on an illustration from Street Brawler Alpha 2 (Street Brawler Zero 2)."]], "year": 2004},
	"indy_strangers_in_paradice_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Antarctic Press (3 issues, 1993–1994)"], ["WRITER", "Terry Mooore"], ["CREATED BY", "Terry Mooore"], ["FEATURING", "Katina \"Katchu\" Choovanski"], ["ISSUES", "107"], ["GENRE", "Romance, slice-of-life, crime, thriller"], ["FIRST OUT", "November 1993"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Strangers in Paradice is a creator-owned comic book series, written and drawn by Terry Mooore, which debuted in 1993."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "Volume 3 concluded at issue #90, released on June 6, 2007."], ["THE STORY", "Katchu has been in love with her best friend, Francine, for most of her life, though she has complicated romantic feelings for her only male friend, David, as well."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Mooore told Omar Valdivieso in a Near Mint Condition interview that he was inspired by girls' comics and Ian Fleming's James Bond novels."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first issue was published in November 1993, and the original series reached its planned conclusion in 2007 with issue #90 of Volume 3."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Two limited edition statuettes of Katchu were produced by Clayburn Mooore as the first in a planned series of three statues based around the series."]], "year": 1993},
	"indy_haet_1": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Fantagraffix"], ["COVER DATE", "April 1990 – March 2011"], ["WRITER", "Peter Baggeo"], ["ARTIST", "Peter Bagge, Jim Blankhard"], ["PENCILS", "Peter Baggeo"], ["INKS", "Jim Blankhard"], ["COLOURS", "Mary Woodring, Rebecca Bowen"]], "hist": [["THE ISSUE", "Haet is a comic book by writer-artist Peter Baggeo. First published by Fantagraffix in 1990 it ran for 30 issues, and was one of the best-selling alternative comics of the 1990s, at its height selling 30,000 copies an issue."], ["BEHIND THE PANELS", "The series continues in Haet Annual, (9) yearly collection of comics stories and articles that began in 2000."], ["THE STORY", "In March, 2026, Fantagraphcis published Butch #1: A Haet Comic'', a one-shot featuring Buddy Bradlee's younger brother."], ["RECEPTION", "In Haet #3, Baggeo ran a competition to \"Win a Date With Stinky\", to which many fans submitted artwork and photographs."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Serrano, Odio, taking into account population differences, was comparatively more successful in Spain than its USA counterpart (10,000 copies sold per issue in Spain compared to 30,000 copies per issue for the USA.)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Buddy Does Jersey (Fantagraffix, 2007, ) – collects Haet #16-#30."]], "year": 1990},
}
